WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.609 --> 00:00:06.000
- Hi everyone.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.368
I'm Saad Omer, I'm the director

00:00:07.368 --> 00:00:09.179
of the Yale Institute for Global Health.

00:00:09.179 --> 00:00:11.050
For those of you who don't know me,

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:12.312
I'm very delighted to welcome you

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:16.729
to this virtual discussion,
focused on updating folks

00:00:17.996 --> 00:00:21.772
on the recent outbreak of monkeypox.

00:00:21.772 --> 00:00:24.727
Obviously the phenomenon is recent,

00:00:24.727 --> 00:00:29.060
and these sets of outbreaks
are attracting attention

00:00:30.076 --> 00:00:32.679
in countries outside
where it has been endemic.

00:00:32.679 --> 00:00:37.512
Monkeypox has been an issue
and has been causing outbreaks

00:00:39.981 --> 00:00:43.514
for a while in several countries.

00:00:43.514 --> 00:00:46.803
Unfortunately, these
things attract attention

00:00:46.803 --> 00:00:48.893
when it spreads more widely,

00:00:48.893 --> 00:00:53.723
especially where there
are vested media markets.

00:00:53.723 --> 00:00:58.262
So keeping that in mind,
so this, which reminds me

00:00:58.262 --> 00:01:01.582
that when you go to the
so-called source of the Nile

00:01:01.582 --> 00:01:04.560
in Uganda, there's a plaque.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:06.589
A plaque that says that it was discovered

00:01:06.589 --> 00:01:09.147
by this and this British explorer,

00:01:09.147 --> 00:01:11.175
and there's a plaque next to it that says,

00:01:11.175 --> 00:01:14.818
"Well, actually we knew
where it was all along.

00:01:14.818 --> 00:01:17.857
This guy showed up and introduced
it to the Western world."

00:01:17.857 --> 00:01:22.309
So keeping that in mind in
terms of monkeypox as well,

00:01:22.309 --> 00:01:26.709
I'm very excited to have
three distinguished guests

00:01:26.709 --> 00:01:29.440
joining us today, all of whom are experts

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:33.489
in infectious diseases and
global health-related topics.

00:01:33.489 --> 00:01:37.060
Before our questions, before we move

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:39.251
to questions and discussions,

00:01:39.251 --> 00:01:42.850
I will introduce each of our panelists.

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:47.850
The first one is Dr. Anne
Rimoin, she's a professor,

00:01:47.956 --> 00:01:49.927
and the Gordon-Levin Endowed Chair

00:01:49.927 --> 00:01:52.029
in Infection Diseases and Public Health

00:01:52.029 --> 00:01:55.544
at the UCLA Fielding
School of Public Health.

00:01:55.544 --> 00:01:58.743
Dr. Rimoin is an internationally
recognized expert

00:01:58.743 --> 00:02:01.224
on emerging infections, global health

00:02:01.224 --> 00:02:03.305
and surveillance systems and vaccination.

00:02:03.305 --> 00:02:07.455
She has been working on
monkeypox before it was cool,

00:02:07.455 --> 00:02:09.788
at least in certain circles.

00:02:10.952 --> 00:02:13.027
I know that it was cool for her all along.

00:02:13.027 --> 00:02:15.346
She did her K award on that,

00:02:15.346 --> 00:02:19.596
and has been working on
this infection for a while.

00:02:22.036 --> 00:02:23.676
Her work has focused on the emergence

00:02:23.676 --> 00:02:25.578
of infectious disease and populations

00:02:25.578 --> 00:02:30.091
living at the intersection
of animal-human contact,

00:02:30.091 --> 00:02:34.591
primarily in the Democratic
Republic of Congo, or DRC.

00:02:36.076 --> 00:02:38.328
Then we have Nate Grubaugh.

00:02:38.328 --> 00:02:39.936
Dr. Nate Grubaugh is
an associate professor

00:02:39.936 --> 00:02:42.766
of Epidemiology of Microbial Diseases

00:02:42.766 --> 00:02:45.173
at the Yale School of Public Health.

00:02:45.173 --> 00:02:47.873
The Grubaugh lab uses
pathogen genomics and

00:02:47.873 --> 00:02:51.782
epidemiological models to
investigate the emergence, spread,

00:02:51.782 --> 00:02:55.614
and evolution of viruses,
such as Zika, dengue,

00:02:55.614 --> 00:02:56.864
and SARS-CoV-2.

00:02:58.295 --> 00:03:01.436
And he is done some very
interesting work on the genomic

00:03:01.436 --> 00:03:04.701
epidemiology of several diseases, but,

00:03:04.701 --> 00:03:07.368
but more recently on SARS-CoV-2.

00:03:09.214 --> 00:03:11.964
And the frames you see behind him

00:03:14.674 --> 00:03:18.893
are titles of the well-deserved
title pages of journals,

00:03:18.893 --> 00:03:23.790
where his, some of his
prominent papers were published.

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:25.328
And, and these are sort of

00:03:25.328 --> 00:03:28.578
well-deserved recognitions of his work.

00:03:29.569 --> 00:03:34.236
Then we have Dr. Akiko Iwasaki,
freshly back from Japan.

00:03:35.455 --> 00:03:38.080
She's a Sterling professor
in more than one ways,

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:40.546
in terms of her professional street cred.

00:03:40.546 --> 00:03:44.552
She is very, her street
cred is very sterling

00:03:44.552 --> 00:03:47.214
in terms of her scientific contributions,

00:03:47.214 --> 00:03:50.493
but she's also the named
Sterling Professor of Immunology

00:03:50.493 --> 00:03:54.827
and Molecular, Cellular &amp;
Developmental Biology at Yale

00:03:54.827 --> 00:03:59.827
and an investigator of the
Howard Hughes Medical Institute.

00:04:00.102 --> 00:04:02.477
Her research focused
focuses on the mechanisms

00:04:02.477 --> 00:04:04.901
of immune defense against viruses

00:04:04.901 --> 00:04:07.190
at the mucosal surfaces, and

00:04:07.190 --> 00:04:10.400
the development of mucosal
vaccine strategies.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:11.524
She's the co lead investigator

00:04:11.524 --> 00:04:13.808
of the Yale COVID-19 recovery study,

00:04:13.808 --> 00:04:18.307
which aims to determine the
changes in immune response of

00:04:18.307 --> 00:04:21.508
people with long COVID after vaccination,

00:04:21.508 --> 00:04:24.066
as the director of the
newly formed Yale Center of

00:04:24.066 --> 00:04:25.983
Infection and Immunity.

00:04:27.992 --> 00:04:30.666
So the way we, you know, those of you

00:04:30.666 --> 00:04:33.599
who are used to joining
our conversation series,

00:04:33.599 --> 00:04:35.475
the format is similar.

00:04:35.475 --> 00:04:40.360
We will have questions and
answers and I will be moderating

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:43.970
for, and I'll start with these questions,

00:04:43.970 --> 00:04:48.818
and then we'll take audience
questions as we go along.

00:04:48.818 --> 00:04:50.318
So the first question,

00:04:50.318 --> 00:04:52.957
and you've been working on monkeypox

00:04:52.957 --> 00:04:54.849
as I said for a long time,

00:04:54.849 --> 00:04:56.999
and we know that the
disease has been increasing

00:04:56.999 --> 00:04:58.436
in recent years in Africa.

00:04:58.436 --> 00:05:01.887
Not only that it has
been around for a while,

00:05:01.887 --> 00:05:03.921
it has been increasing.

00:05:03.921 --> 00:05:06.629
Can you talk about the history
of the disease, or at least

00:05:06.629 --> 00:05:08.211
the known history of the disease

00:05:08.211 --> 00:05:10.544
and recent trends in Africa?

00:05:12.051 --> 00:05:13.242
- Absolutely.

00:05:13.242 --> 00:05:15.443
So Saad, thanks for organizing this.

00:05:15.443 --> 00:05:18.884
I'm so thrilled to be here
with this incredible panel.

00:05:18.884 --> 00:05:21.513
And so let me just tell you a little bit

00:05:21.513 --> 00:05:22.924
about monkeypox in general.

00:05:22.924 --> 00:05:25.052
So monkeypox, just as a kind of

00:05:25.052 --> 00:05:28.012
an encapsulated background
about monkeypox,

00:05:28.012 --> 00:05:31.771
monkeypox was first discovered
in non-human primates,

00:05:31.771 --> 00:05:34.833
in a colony in the Netherlands

00:05:34.833 --> 00:05:36.885
where they were doing polio testing.

00:05:36.885 --> 00:05:39.372
Actually they were
testing the polio vaccine.

00:05:39.372 --> 00:05:41.163
This was in 1958,

00:05:41.163 --> 00:05:42.652
and that's why it's called
monkeypox, because it was

00:05:42.652 --> 00:05:44.909
first discovered in primates.

00:05:44.909 --> 00:05:47.205
"Monkeypox" is actually misnomer.

00:05:47.205 --> 00:05:49.776
It's actually a more of a
"rodent pox" as we know it,

00:05:49.776 --> 00:05:51.576
although we still are trying

00:05:51.576 --> 00:05:55.030
to truly understand
what the host range is.

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:57.804
The first time that monkeypox
was discovered in humans

00:05:57.804 --> 00:06:02.721
was in 1970, in a child in the
democratic Republic of Congo

00:06:03.743 --> 00:06:05.453
who's nine months old.

00:06:05.453 --> 00:06:08.447
And this was in the context
of smallpox eradication.

00:06:08.447 --> 00:06:10.559
So they were doing,

00:06:10.559 --> 00:06:13.099
they were investigating
any case of rash illness

00:06:13.099 --> 00:06:15.720
that they were, that could
have, that could be similar

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:19.803
to smallpox, and doing
virus culture, and really,

00:06:21.217 --> 00:06:24.936
you know, trying to get
down to exactly what it is.

00:06:24.936 --> 00:06:25.769
That's when they discovered that monkeypox

00:06:25.769 --> 00:06:28.518
actually could infect humans.

00:06:28.518 --> 00:06:31.809
Monkeypox, then, they of
course were very concerned

00:06:31.809 --> 00:06:34.009
about a pox virus that
was infecting humans.

00:06:34.009 --> 00:06:36.758
And so they were gonna
follow it very carefully.

00:06:36.758 --> 00:06:40.418
They started doing intensive
disease surveillance in DRC,

00:06:40.418 --> 00:06:42.427
and they did find that there
were cases of monkeypox

00:06:42.427 --> 00:06:44.078
that were occurring, although,

00:06:44.078 --> 00:06:47.216
because smallpox provides
excellent protect...

00:06:47.216 --> 00:06:49.454
Smallpox vaccine, and natural infection

00:06:49.454 --> 00:06:53.999
provides excellent immunity
to other pox viruses,

00:06:53.999 --> 00:06:55.320
which is really the whole idea

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:57.037
of smallpox vaccine anyway,

00:06:57.037 --> 00:06:59.347
I'm sure you guys all know the
history of smallpox vaccine.

00:06:59.347 --> 00:07:03.930
It's not smallpox, it's a
cowpox, horse pox, something.

00:07:04.848 --> 00:07:06.507
We don't really know that, what vaccine,

00:07:06.507 --> 00:07:07.965
that's a whole different question,

00:07:07.965 --> 00:07:10.202
what vaccine it actually is.

00:07:10.202 --> 00:07:13.315
But, it provides protection
against smallpox.

00:07:13.315 --> 00:07:16.982
So, the time that we
really understood most,

00:07:17.988 --> 00:07:22.238
the most about monkeypox,
was from 1981 until 1986.

00:07:24.026 --> 00:07:26.388
So just after smallpox was
eradicated, they thought,

00:07:26.388 --> 00:07:28.326
"hey, we better really
understand about the,

00:07:28.326 --> 00:07:31.676
what this pox virus that's
circulating in humans does."

00:07:31.676 --> 00:07:34.613
So they did intensified
disease surveillance in DRC,

00:07:34.613 --> 00:07:37.536
to truly understand how it was spreading.

00:07:37.536 --> 00:07:41.277
And they did this in several health zones.

00:07:41.277 --> 00:07:42.546
So it wasn't throughout
the entire country.

00:07:42.546 --> 00:07:44.662
It was just in very specific
places where they knew there

00:07:44.662 --> 00:07:47.464
were cases, they found
somewhere around 338 cases,

00:07:47.464 --> 00:07:49.783
they investigated them carefully.

00:07:49.783 --> 00:07:52.008
And, you know, that's
really what the base,

00:07:52.008 --> 00:07:56.765
the bulk of monkeypox epidemiology
clinical presentation,

00:07:56.765 --> 00:07:58.875
like this work is really predicated

00:07:58.875 --> 00:08:01.626
on this work from 1981 to 1986,

00:08:01.626 --> 00:08:03.786
where they did intensified
disease surveillance.

00:08:03.786 --> 00:08:07.194
They were giving what's
equivalent to like $90 a person

00:08:07.194 --> 00:08:09.675
for any report of monkeypox.

00:08:09.675 --> 00:08:11.502
So they were really doing
a lot of case investing.

00:08:11.502 --> 00:08:14.453
You could imagine, 1980s, what $90,

00:08:14.453 --> 00:08:16.546
I mean, even today, $90,
it's a lot of money.

00:08:16.546 --> 00:08:18.705
So there was a lot of incentive.

00:08:18.705 --> 00:08:20.574
People really knew what
to look for at that time.

00:08:20.574 --> 00:08:22.995
So there was a lot better
disease surveillance ongoing,

00:08:22.995 --> 00:08:26.282
and that's how they found these cases.

00:08:26.282 --> 00:08:27.854
They did a lot of modeling as a result.

00:08:27.854 --> 00:08:29.525
They did a lot of ecological
studies, and what they

00:08:29.525 --> 00:08:32.553
determined at that time was
that monkeypox was, you know,

00:08:32.553 --> 00:08:36.883
it had limited human-to-human
spread, that most cases

00:08:36.883 --> 00:08:39.513
were spillover from animals.

00:08:39.513 --> 00:08:43.930
They only were able to
isolate it in a single animal,

00:08:45.002 --> 00:08:48.361
I think it was a squirrel, in 1984.

00:08:48.361 --> 00:08:52.585
And by the way, it hasn't
ever been isolated again,

00:08:52.585 --> 00:08:54.992
except for one other
time in the early 2000s,

00:08:54.992 --> 00:08:57.325
in an animal in west Africa.

00:08:58.962 --> 00:09:01.441
But what they just determined
was that this really wasn't

00:09:01.441 --> 00:09:03.914
gonna be something that
was going to spread.

00:09:03.914 --> 00:09:07.173
And the reason, and if you
look at these early papers,

00:09:07.173 --> 00:09:09.986
what they say is,
something to the effect of,

00:09:09.986 --> 00:09:11.196
and this is paraphrase.

00:09:11.196 --> 00:09:13.544
But, because what we're going to see is

00:09:13.544 --> 00:09:15.933
increased urbanization, we're going to see

00:09:15.933 --> 00:09:18.653
improved healthcare, we're
going to see, you know.

00:09:18.653 --> 00:09:20.104
This was the early 80s, right?

00:09:20.104 --> 00:09:22.934
I mean, the early 80s, we really thought

00:09:22.934 --> 00:09:25.354
that life was gonna be
very, very different.

00:09:25.354 --> 00:09:28.144
There was a very rosy outlook
for what was going to be

00:09:28.144 --> 00:09:29.914
happening in all of these places.

00:09:29.914 --> 00:09:31.255
And in particular, in a place like DRC.

00:09:31.255 --> 00:09:34.444
So increased urbanization,
increased travel,

00:09:34.444 --> 00:09:39.252
more reliance on commercial
food products and egg

00:09:39.252 --> 00:09:40.616
as opposed to bush meat.

00:09:40.616 --> 00:09:42.746
So therefore we think that the probability

00:09:42.746 --> 00:09:45.608
of it spilling over is going
to be less and less over time.

00:09:45.608 --> 00:09:48.454
But of course, a place like
DRC, that's not what happened.

00:09:48.454 --> 00:09:50.676
What happened was we had a complete

00:09:50.676 --> 00:09:52.438
destruction of the health system.

00:09:52.438 --> 00:09:55.038
There was prolonged civil war,

00:09:55.038 --> 00:09:57.697
people were forced into the forests.

00:09:57.697 --> 00:10:00.565
We saw a lot more hunting of the forest.

00:10:00.565 --> 00:10:01.806
The forest became hunted out,

00:10:01.806 --> 00:10:03.715
people were eating a lot more rodents.

00:10:03.715 --> 00:10:06.036
People, you know, were malnourished.

00:10:06.036 --> 00:10:07.458
You saw all of these comorbidities.

00:10:07.458 --> 00:10:09.475
And so that's really, we saw, in fact,

00:10:09.475 --> 00:10:12.488
so I, along with colleagues in 2010,

00:10:12.488 --> 00:10:13.977
when we started our monkeypox projects,

00:10:13.977 --> 00:10:16.618
really saw this incredible
increase in cases.

00:10:16.618 --> 00:10:19.018
It was totally unexpected,
and really different

00:10:19.018 --> 00:10:21.785
from what they had predicted.

00:10:21.785 --> 00:10:24.526
So, you know, that just goes to show you,

00:10:24.526 --> 00:10:26.556
you always have to be
updating your priors, right?

00:10:26.556 --> 00:10:29.418
I mean, that's what we
do, in epidemiology is,

00:10:29.418 --> 00:10:32.506
we're always thinking about,
what are our assumptions

00:10:32.506 --> 00:10:34.256
that we're making, and
how are things changing?

00:10:34.256 --> 00:10:36.677
So we had a change in
epidemiologic landscape.

00:10:36.677 --> 00:10:39.245
We had a change in ecologic landscape.

00:10:39.245 --> 00:10:42.124
We've seen population explosion over time.

00:10:42.124 --> 00:10:45.983
We've now seen cases
occur in Nigeria as well.

00:10:45.983 --> 00:10:50.234
And that outbreak has continued over time.

00:10:50.234 --> 00:10:51.857
And so, you know,

00:10:51.857 --> 00:10:56.135
really where we are today is
that most of our knowledge is

00:10:56.135 --> 00:10:59.233
about this from the studies
that were done in the 80s.

00:10:59.233 --> 00:11:03.472
I had a study in DRC, UC
Emirate had a study in DRC,

00:11:03.472 --> 00:11:04.353
CDC had a study.

00:11:04.353 --> 00:11:08.804
These were very limited scope
studies with limited funding.

00:11:08.804 --> 00:11:11.263
So we have pieces of information,

00:11:11.263 --> 00:11:14.012
but we really know about it
in rural hunting villages

00:11:14.012 --> 00:11:16.136
in central Africa.

00:11:16.136 --> 00:11:19.023
The outbreak in 2017 was
really a wake up call,

00:11:19.023 --> 00:11:20.995
I think, to everybody,
that monkeypox, you know,

00:11:20.995 --> 00:11:24.212
we could be seeing an ecologic change,

00:11:24.212 --> 00:11:26.204
that the host range had changed.

00:11:26.204 --> 00:11:27.553
You know, it wasn't recognized quickly.

00:11:27.553 --> 00:11:31.220
I always draw the example
of West, of Ebola.

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:34.883
You know, you have something
kind of smoldering around,

00:11:34.883 --> 00:11:36.834
like in Guinea, nobody recognizes it.

00:11:36.834 --> 00:11:39.513
All of a sudden, you see it explode.

00:11:39.513 --> 00:11:44.345
And so what we've now seen,
our cases just increase over,

00:11:44.345 --> 00:11:45.895
you know, there was
already an outbreak ongoing

00:11:45.895 --> 00:11:47.656
by the time they recognized it.

00:11:47.656 --> 00:11:49.607
We've seen changes in
clinical presentation,

00:11:49.607 --> 00:11:51.030
we've seen all these other things.

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:52.849
Nigeria is an economic hub.

00:11:52.849 --> 00:11:56.518
And so as a result, we started
to see exportation of cases.

00:11:56.518 --> 00:11:59.726
We had a number of cases
exported that we knew about,

00:11:59.726 --> 00:12:00.657
of course, this could have been happening

00:12:00.657 --> 00:12:04.074
for a long time, between 2018 to present.

00:12:05.237 --> 00:12:06.677
And of course, that brings us to today,

00:12:06.677 --> 00:12:10.844
which, now we have a global
outbreak of monkeypox.

00:12:12.544 --> 00:12:14.855
- Yeah. So you mentioned that, you know,

00:12:14.855 --> 00:12:16.119
who would've thought that we'd be talking

00:12:16.119 --> 00:12:18.427
about pox viruses, et cetera.

00:12:18.427 --> 00:12:20.220
And it is, for this conversation series,

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:23.407
thought you'd be interested
to know that our last guest,

00:12:23.407 --> 00:12:28.008
which we thought was the end
of our semester of, you know,

00:12:28.008 --> 00:12:30.439
of our conversation
series for the semester,

00:12:30.439 --> 00:12:34.508
was Dr. Bill Foege, who
devised the strategy

00:12:34.508 --> 00:12:38.659
to eradicate smallpox and
co-led the program, et cetera.

00:12:38.659 --> 00:12:42.046
So to do, I wouldn't have
thought even in January

00:12:42.046 --> 00:12:45.828
that we'd be talking about
two pox viruses back to back.

00:12:45.828 --> 00:12:49.495
So one thing to remind
everyone that we are,

00:12:51.149 --> 00:12:52.249
you can post your questions in

00:12:52.249 --> 00:12:56.047
the question and answer
section, and we'd be happy to,

00:12:56.047 --> 00:12:57.700
after a series of questions that I ask,

00:12:57.700 --> 00:12:59.929
we'd be happy to ask these questions

00:12:59.929 --> 00:13:02.786
on your behalf from the panel members.

00:13:02.786 --> 00:13:07.087
So Nate, and sort of the next
question is from you and,

00:13:07.087 --> 00:13:11.214
Anne started talking about that initially.

00:13:11.214 --> 00:13:13.138
Do you think, has something changed

00:13:13.138 --> 00:13:16.471
about the virus in the way it is spread?

00:13:17.399 --> 00:13:19.985
What can virus genomics tell us

00:13:19.985 --> 00:13:23.438
about how it is evolving and spreading?

00:13:23.438 --> 00:13:26.023
- Okay, well, thanks
Saad for the invitation

00:13:26.023 --> 00:13:28.272
to join this panel, I primarily joined

00:13:28.272 --> 00:13:31.154
so I can get a front row
seat to hear from you all.

00:13:31.154 --> 00:13:35.715
And hopefully I can have
something here to contribute.

00:13:35.715 --> 00:13:40.487
So, you know, there's now
about, what, a thousand or so

00:13:40.487 --> 00:13:45.438
suspected in firm cases that,
in non-endemic countries.

00:13:45.438 --> 00:13:48.188
And, so far with the patterns and

00:13:49.451 --> 00:13:51.419
in contact history of the cases,

00:13:51.419 --> 00:13:53.800
it's supporting
human-to-human transmission

00:13:53.800 --> 00:13:57.050
that Anne had mentioned that can occur.

00:13:57.891 --> 00:14:00.309
And, but we don't really
know yet if anything

00:14:00.309 --> 00:14:03.991
is substantially changed about the virus,

00:14:03.991 --> 00:14:05.649
especially to the extent that maybe

00:14:05.649 --> 00:14:07.726
that it's respiratory born.

00:14:07.726 --> 00:14:09.838
I don't know how much of
that is actually known,

00:14:09.838 --> 00:14:12.259
and it seems like, you know,

00:14:12.259 --> 00:14:14.197
primarily right now
with a lot of the cases,

00:14:14.197 --> 00:14:17.925
that associated with close contact.

00:14:17.925 --> 00:14:21.177
So the genomics can
provide some evidence here

00:14:21.177 --> 00:14:22.557
to support some of those,

00:14:22.557 --> 00:14:25.140
and allow us to further dig in.

00:14:26.008 --> 00:14:29.697
First is that most of the sequenced cases

00:14:29.697 --> 00:14:32.649
belong to one major clade,

00:14:32.649 --> 00:14:35.348
that is a part of this
West African strain,

00:14:35.348 --> 00:14:39.205
which is thought to be less
virulent, and so far, I guess,

00:14:39.205 --> 00:14:41.118
supporting that is that we haven't had

00:14:41.118 --> 00:14:42.798
very many severe cases.

00:14:42.798 --> 00:14:44.897
And, and I don't think
any deaths yet associated,

00:14:44.897 --> 00:14:46.728
at least in the non-endemic countries

00:14:46.728 --> 00:14:50.679
as a part of this, you
know, 2022 outbreak.

00:14:50.679 --> 00:14:54.388
But some have hypothesized
that there might be

00:14:54.388 --> 00:14:57.148
virus adaptations that
is associated with these,

00:14:57.148 --> 00:15:00.818
this clade that may be
aiding the outbreak.

00:15:00.818 --> 00:15:03.885
However, recently, the CDC sequenced

00:15:03.885 --> 00:15:07.789
some other viruses that are
also still West African clade,

00:15:07.789 --> 00:15:10.819
but there are different
lineages sort of within that

00:15:10.819 --> 00:15:13.156
and not this main one.

00:15:13.156 --> 00:15:15.939
So to me that suggests that
there's probably not something

00:15:15.939 --> 00:15:20.217
special about that main clade
that is causing the outbreak.

00:15:20.217 --> 00:15:22.508
It's, probably, it's the most common

00:15:22.508 --> 00:15:24.886
causing human-to-human transmission

00:15:24.886 --> 00:15:29.136
in the endemic regions, in
West and Central Africa.

00:15:31.154 --> 00:15:33.789
But, you know, as someone
who really likes genomics

00:15:33.789 --> 00:15:36.138
and likes to nerd out
on some of this stuff,

00:15:36.138 --> 00:15:38.709
some of the findings have
been really interesting.

00:15:38.709 --> 00:15:42.126
So monkeypox virus is this big DNA virus.

00:15:43.719 --> 00:15:45.867
And it evolves pretty slowly,

00:15:45.867 --> 00:15:48.819
at one to two mutations per year,

00:15:48.819 --> 00:15:50.839
because it has these
proofreading mechanisms

00:15:50.839 --> 00:15:54.609
a lot of the RNA viruses
that I study don't have.

00:15:54.609 --> 00:15:55.959
But there's some interesting signs

00:15:55.959 --> 00:15:58.670
of accelerated evolution
that has occurred.

00:15:58.670 --> 00:16:02.359
So this branch leading up to
the major clade of viruses

00:16:02.359 --> 00:16:03.671
that have been sequenced

00:16:03.671 --> 00:16:07.098
has about 40 mutations that
accumulated over four years.

00:16:07.098 --> 00:16:10.230
It's about 10 times more
than what we thought.

00:16:10.230 --> 00:16:14.423
But some terrific sleuthing
by colleagues have pointed out

00:16:14.423 --> 00:16:16.695
that a lot of these
mutational patterns actually

00:16:16.695 --> 00:16:20.216
don't really match the normal
way that viruses mutate,

00:16:20.216 --> 00:16:23.029
which is when you have, when
the virus is replicating,

00:16:23.029 --> 00:16:25.095
it makes some mistakes here and there,

00:16:25.095 --> 00:16:26.686
and they happen throughout the genome.

00:16:26.686 --> 00:16:28.227
And some of them are advantageous,

00:16:28.227 --> 00:16:32.026
but most of them don't
necessarily help the virus.

00:16:32.026 --> 00:16:34.685
These mutations actually
look like patterns

00:16:34.685 --> 00:16:37.506
that are introduced by
the host immune response.

00:16:37.506 --> 00:16:41.957
These enzymes called
APOBECs that are known,

00:16:41.957 --> 00:16:43.435
I think probably most known

00:16:43.435 --> 00:16:45.938
for their control of HIV infections.

00:16:45.938 --> 00:16:49.615
And so these mutations
that have accumulated

00:16:49.615 --> 00:16:52.765
might actually be more
of "scars" on the virus

00:16:52.765 --> 00:16:56.587
left by the host than true
mutations or signs of adaptation.

00:16:56.587 --> 00:16:58.474
But to me, what it suggests, that there's

00:16:58.474 --> 00:16:59.795
some extended period of time

00:16:59.795 --> 00:17:03.015
to human transmission that has occurred.

00:17:03.015 --> 00:17:05.525
And, that's what we're
seeing with these scars.

00:17:05.525 --> 00:17:08.093
So it, to me this indicates
that there's probably

00:17:08.093 --> 00:17:09.927
a much larger outbreak happening

00:17:09.927 --> 00:17:14.057
in West and Central Africa
than what was realized.

00:17:14.057 --> 00:17:15.935
And we're seeing the extent of that now

00:17:15.935 --> 00:17:19.316
through cases popping
up in other countries.

00:17:19.316 --> 00:17:21.746
I would really like to get
Dr. Rimoin's take on that

00:17:21.746 --> 00:17:23.087
to see if she agrees with me,

00:17:23.087 --> 00:17:25.606
I see her nodding, so that's helpful.

00:17:25.606 --> 00:17:28.657
So with the genomics, there's
still a ton to unpack here.

00:17:28.657 --> 00:17:30.136
We can use it to help understand

00:17:30.136 --> 00:17:32.268
if transmission in different locations,

00:17:32.268 --> 00:17:35.257
if they're connected, and to
help us to develop hypotheses,

00:17:35.257 --> 00:17:37.367
that we can both test
with clinical studies

00:17:37.367 --> 00:17:40.374
and with experimental studies.

00:17:40.374 --> 00:17:41.695
- You know, that's interesting.

00:17:41.695 --> 00:17:46.695
And one of the things is
that there's always the cost

00:17:46.832 --> 00:17:50.915
for flying blind, and it's
in our shared interest

00:17:54.835 --> 00:17:57.804
to have investments in high quality

00:17:57.804 --> 00:18:00.695
epidemiologic and lab surveillance.

00:18:00.695 --> 00:18:04.033
And the facts that these
investments were not made.

00:18:04.033 --> 00:18:07.626
So, as an example, where was
the level of coronavirus,

00:18:07.626 --> 00:18:11.634
seasonal coronavirus
research before the pandemic?

00:18:11.634 --> 00:18:16.034
A parallel is flu vaccine,
and flu virus research.

00:18:16.034 --> 00:18:17.871
We didn't invest in, and frankly,

00:18:17.871 --> 00:18:20.913
a lot of that ways, the
variants are behaving, you know,

00:18:20.913 --> 00:18:24.353
there's an overlap, not
exactly the same thing,

00:18:24.353 --> 00:18:27.692
the behaving in an exaggerated fashion of

00:18:27.692 --> 00:18:30.461
seasonal coronavirus, just as
an example, roughly stated.

00:18:30.461 --> 00:18:34.123
So we could have learned
a lot of things from that.

00:18:34.123 --> 00:18:38.220
Similarly, if we had had
investments in surveillance

00:18:38.220 --> 00:18:42.620
and both epidemiological
and lab surveillance

00:18:42.620 --> 00:18:47.620
in terms of coronaviruses, in
terms of, sorry, monkeypox,

00:18:48.169 --> 00:18:51.049
we would have been better prepared for it

00:18:51.049 --> 00:18:53.272
for the sake of countries
where it is endemic,

00:18:53.272 --> 00:18:56.885
and for other countries
that are facing it now.

00:18:56.885 --> 00:19:00.385
So the next question I have is from Akiko.

00:19:01.312 --> 00:19:03.370
So what do you know, what do we know

00:19:03.370 --> 00:19:06.000
about the immunology
of the monkeypox virus,

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:08.638
and how long smallpox vaccine protection

00:19:08.638 --> 00:19:11.070
is expected to last?

00:19:11.070 --> 00:19:14.329
What has happened to
our population immunity

00:19:14.329 --> 00:19:16.770
since the smallpox vaccination campaigns

00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:18.770
ended after eradication?

00:19:19.969 --> 00:19:22.168
- Yeah, thank you Saad for the questions,

00:19:22.168 --> 00:19:24.358
and for having me on this panel too.

00:19:24.358 --> 00:19:26.187
I, like Nate, I'm sitting here

00:19:26.187 --> 00:19:29.409
in order to try to learn
from the other experts

00:19:29.409 --> 00:19:30.657
about what they have to say,

00:19:30.657 --> 00:19:33.736
and I'd be happy to come
back to this APOBEC issue

00:19:33.736 --> 00:19:37.959
if there's time later (laughs)
and no doubt about that, too.

00:19:37.959 --> 00:19:39.890
So, yeah, getting back
to Saad, your question.

00:19:39.890 --> 00:19:43.799
So pox viruses in general,
infections in general,

00:19:43.799 --> 00:19:48.690
induce pretty robust and long
term immunity against disease.

00:19:48.690 --> 00:19:51.650
So smallpox vaccine as
Anne already described

00:19:51.650 --> 00:19:54.959
is a live virus related to the smallpox

00:19:54.959 --> 00:19:57.879
called vaccinia virus,
that normally infects

00:19:57.879 --> 00:20:00.598
other animals like cows.

00:20:00.598 --> 00:20:03.609
So while the CDC website
says that the vaccination

00:20:03.609 --> 00:20:06.610
with the vaccinia and virus
provides full immunity

00:20:06.610 --> 00:20:09.901
to three to five years,
I believe that the data

00:20:09.901 --> 00:20:11.540
are more consistent with the immunity

00:20:11.540 --> 00:20:13.745
lasting for a lot longer,

00:20:13.745 --> 00:20:17.218
even in those who were
only vaccinated once.

00:20:17.218 --> 00:20:19.848
So historically the
vaccine has been effective

00:20:19.848 --> 00:20:22.959
in preventing smallpox
in like, 95% of those

00:20:22.959 --> 00:20:26.080
who are vaccinated, and
against the monkeypox

00:20:26.080 --> 00:20:30.315
there's about 85% protection
against the disease.

00:20:30.315 --> 00:20:33.418
In addition, because pox
viruses replicate slowly,

00:20:33.418 --> 00:20:35.748
the vaccine can be, are proven to be

00:20:35.748 --> 00:20:39.028
substantially able to lessen the disease

00:20:39.028 --> 00:20:42.503
when given within a few days of exposure.

00:20:42.503 --> 00:20:45.541
And the correlate of
protection is well understood

00:20:45.541 --> 00:20:48.870
also for the smallpox at least,

00:20:48.870 --> 00:20:52.440
the neutralizing antibody
titers above 1 in 32

00:20:52.440 --> 00:20:54.362
are associated with protective immunity

00:20:54.362 --> 00:20:56.939
against the smallpox disease.

00:20:56.939 --> 00:20:58.341
And T-cells also contribute,

00:20:58.341 --> 00:21:02.172
but antibodies are sufficient
to confer protection.

00:21:02.172 --> 00:21:05.321
So the routine smallpox vaccines
among the American public

00:21:05.321 --> 00:21:10.321
stopped in 1972, after the
disease was eradicated in the US.

00:21:10.772 --> 00:21:13.524
In Japan where I grew up,
the public vaccination

00:21:13.524 --> 00:21:17.683
against smallpox also ended
in 1976, and consequently,

00:21:17.683 --> 00:21:21.015
I only received one dose of the vaccine.

00:21:21.015 --> 00:21:24.434
But in the study published in 2008,

00:21:24.434 --> 00:21:27.443
the researchers found that in 97%

00:21:27.443 --> 00:21:30.492
of the participants that
they've investigated,

00:21:30.492 --> 00:21:35.043
there is no decrease
vaccinia-specific antibody titers

00:21:35.043 --> 00:21:36.591
that would've noted with age

00:21:36.591 --> 00:21:39.609
over a follow up period of 88 years.

00:21:39.609 --> 00:21:41.623
That's pretty remarkable.

00:21:41.623 --> 00:21:43.623
Another study found that T-cell responses

00:21:43.623 --> 00:21:47.339
after vaccinia vaccine last for 30 years.

00:21:47.339 --> 00:21:49.924
So we can expect significant level

00:21:49.924 --> 00:21:52.920
protection against disease,
in those who have been

00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:57.531
previously vaccinated
with the vaccinia vaccine,

00:21:57.531 --> 00:22:00.220
and hopefully the same kind of protection

00:22:00.220 --> 00:22:02.387
would apply for monkeypox.

00:22:05.140 --> 00:22:07.500
- So a related sort of question,

00:22:07.500 --> 00:22:09.962
and actually I'll take a
question from the audience

00:22:09.962 --> 00:22:11.989
because it's related, sort of,

00:22:11.989 --> 00:22:15.451
kind of earlier than we planned to.

00:22:15.451 --> 00:22:18.713
So this question is from Edward Arenberg.

00:22:18.713 --> 00:22:23.221
Is there any heterologous,
and this is to you, Akiko,

00:22:23.221 --> 00:22:24.590
do you think, and are you aware of,

00:22:24.590 --> 00:22:27.648
any heterologous protection
against monkeypox virus,

00:22:27.648 --> 00:22:30.398
from molluscum contagiosum virus?

00:22:31.630 --> 00:22:33.932
You know, any thoughts on that, look,

00:22:33.932 --> 00:22:36.172
if early in the outbreak,
We should be asking

00:22:36.172 --> 00:22:38.523
broad questions.
- [Anne] Yeah, sort of.

00:22:38.523 --> 00:22:41.499
- Because, and then sort of exploring

00:22:41.499 --> 00:22:43.051
these kinds of hypotheses.

00:22:43.051 --> 00:22:45.557
Obviously this is not "purely"
early in the outbreak,

00:22:45.557 --> 00:22:48.138
but in this phase of the outbreak.

00:22:48.138 --> 00:22:51.299
- Yeah, so I have no data about that,

00:22:51.299 --> 00:22:54.837
but really depends on how
related these viruses are.

00:22:54.837 --> 00:22:59.549
And, Anne, if you have any
insights, please jump in.

00:22:59.549 --> 00:23:00.659
- Well, I think that,

00:23:00.659 --> 00:23:02.880
so I think that this is a
really interesting question.

00:23:02.880 --> 00:23:05.170
I don't know about molluscum contagiosum.

00:23:05.170 --> 00:23:06.003
I don't know.

00:23:06.003 --> 00:23:06.836
And it's a good question.

00:23:06.836 --> 00:23:08.428
I literally was just
writing it down saying, oh,

00:23:08.428 --> 00:23:09.631
I think that's an interesting question.

00:23:09.631 --> 00:23:11.921
Mark Slifka might be a good
person to ask about this

00:23:11.921 --> 00:23:14.249
because he's the person who, in 2008,

00:23:14.249 --> 00:23:16.550
I believe it was his paper
you were talking about,

00:23:16.550 --> 00:23:18.533
he did some of that early work.

00:23:18.533 --> 00:23:20.475
But you know, what it made me think about,

00:23:20.475 --> 00:23:22.501
and these are just thoughts
that I'm having, you know,

00:23:22.501 --> 00:23:24.315
and this is all part of the
scientific process, right?

00:23:24.315 --> 00:23:26.442
This is why it's so
great that you guys are

00:23:26.442 --> 00:23:28.175
getting all of us
together to start talking

00:23:28.175 --> 00:23:30.265
about these things, 'cause
this is how science is done,

00:23:30.265 --> 00:23:32.053
and these kind of questions come up.

00:23:32.053 --> 00:23:33.585
So when you were talking
about this and thinking,

00:23:33.585 --> 00:23:35.396
okay, well, you know,
T-cell responses last

00:23:35.396 --> 00:23:37.832
for about 30 years, we have
long term protection, you know,

00:23:37.832 --> 00:23:41.846
I had a paper in PNAS in
2010 where I demonstrated

00:23:41.846 --> 00:23:44.174
this long term rise in, or this,

00:23:44.174 --> 00:23:48.223
this extensive rise in cases
of monkeypox since the 1980s,

00:23:48.223 --> 00:23:53.213
and really correlated it with
the cessation of vaccination.

00:23:53.213 --> 00:23:55.133
And we really had almost
no cases in individuals

00:23:55.133 --> 00:23:59.087
who had been vaccinated at that point.

00:23:59.087 --> 00:24:00.784
But what it makes me
think about in general is,

00:24:00.784 --> 00:24:03.284
what is the threshold at which

00:24:04.195 --> 00:24:07.527
we really start to see
this, and will we need,

00:24:07.527 --> 00:24:09.234
and I'm just, these are just questions

00:24:09.234 --> 00:24:10.845
that come into my head.

00:24:10.845 --> 00:24:12.322
You know, if we think that there's like

00:24:12.322 --> 00:24:14.365
a certain threshold of immunity

00:24:14.365 --> 00:24:18.252
that we need to maintain
to be able to avoid this,

00:24:18.252 --> 00:24:19.543
I mean, of course what you really wanna do

00:24:19.543 --> 00:24:21.844
is you wanna be able to do that in places

00:24:21.844 --> 00:24:24.144
where monkeypox is endemic right now.

00:24:24.144 --> 00:24:26.756
Because it's always better
to control it at the source.

00:24:26.756 --> 00:24:28.431
And this goes back to what Saad was saying

00:24:28.431 --> 00:24:30.733
at the very beginning, which is, you know,

00:24:30.733 --> 00:24:33.237
we pay the price every single time

00:24:33.237 --> 00:24:36.985
that we don't invest in
disease surveillance,

00:24:36.985 --> 00:24:40.145
lab surveillance, and this
kind of infrastructure.

00:24:40.145 --> 00:24:41.743
And as my late father-in-law
always used to say,

00:24:41.743 --> 00:24:43.663
it's much easier to stay out of trouble

00:24:43.663 --> 00:24:46.901
than it is to have to get out of trouble.

00:24:46.901 --> 00:24:49.344
And I really think that that's, you know,

00:24:49.344 --> 00:24:54.344
it just couldn't be more
true than in public health.

00:24:54.423 --> 00:24:55.999
I mean, that's really the point.

00:24:55.999 --> 00:24:58.132
But I just start to wonder,
well, what is that threshold?

00:24:58.132 --> 00:25:02.382
And maybe we'll have to
reach that threshold again,

00:25:03.302 --> 00:25:08.135
either by natural, or preferably
vaccine induced immunity,

00:25:09.603 --> 00:25:13.159
by re-vaccinating
certain groups to be able

00:25:13.159 --> 00:25:16.409
to really achieve that threshold again.

00:25:17.904 --> 00:25:21.323
In particular, if we
start to see this virus

00:25:21.323 --> 00:25:24.264
establish itself in human, as
something that is spreading

00:25:24.264 --> 00:25:27.233
even at a low level amongst humans,

00:25:27.233 --> 00:25:30.243
or my big concern is
if we see a spill back

00:25:30.243 --> 00:25:31.993
or reverse humanosis.

00:25:33.505 --> 00:25:35.293
You know, then we're really
gonna have to consider

00:25:35.293 --> 00:25:37.841
what does it take to be able
to keep pox viruses at bay?

00:25:37.841 --> 00:25:41.047
And this then goes back to
what Nathan was talking about,

00:25:41.047 --> 00:25:44.584
which is, what do we think
about these genetic changes

00:25:44.584 --> 00:25:47.243
and what does this mean?

00:25:47.243 --> 00:25:49.271
You know, pox viruses are stable viruses.

00:25:49.271 --> 00:25:53.771
We don't see a lot of, we
really don't see the kind of

00:25:55.208 --> 00:25:59.322
potential for adaptation
and fitness that we do,

00:25:59.322 --> 00:26:01.461
for example, the coronavirus.

00:26:01.461 --> 00:26:03.142
That said, we do see changes.

00:26:03.142 --> 00:26:04.161
Do we know what they mean?

00:26:04.161 --> 00:26:05.362
What will they mean?

00:26:05.362 --> 00:26:07.272
We, you know, these are things
we need to get in front of,

00:26:07.272 --> 00:26:10.772
as opposed to chasing behind them.

00:26:10.772 --> 00:26:13.901
- Yeah, no, so, I have, I also have,

00:26:13.901 --> 00:26:15.422
I was born outside the US.

00:26:15.422 --> 00:26:19.533
I have a smallpox scar,
because, you know, in the,

00:26:19.533 --> 00:26:21.163
a lot of parts of the world, they,

00:26:21.163 --> 00:26:24.580
that vaccination was stopped a bit later.

00:26:27.102 --> 00:26:31.199
But we should remember, look,
these countries where even,

00:26:31.199 --> 00:26:33.041
you know, vaccination was stopped later,

00:26:33.041 --> 00:26:36.472
have really, really young populations.

00:26:36.472 --> 00:26:38.141
And so according to some estimates,

00:26:38.141 --> 00:26:41.238
even if under the best case scenario,

00:26:41.238 --> 00:26:45.072
the population immunity
provided by a smallpox vaccine

00:26:45.072 --> 00:26:48.130
is around a quarter of the population.

00:26:48.130 --> 00:26:51.731
Because of the, just the
population pyramid and even,

00:26:51.731 --> 00:26:54.814
and the fact that with smallpox only,

00:26:56.882 --> 00:27:00.122
or not only, 85% protection is high.

00:27:00.122 --> 00:27:02.371
So even if that kind of a scenario.

00:27:02.371 --> 00:27:05.232
So we should keep in mind, it's
not just the immunity part,

00:27:05.232 --> 00:27:10.232
but also the demography in
some of those countries.

00:27:10.251 --> 00:27:13.501
So coming back to Nate, what convinced,

00:27:15.322 --> 00:27:16.222
what can we do...

00:27:16.222 --> 00:27:20.959
Actually, you know what, I'll
start with you again, Anne.

00:27:20.959 --> 00:27:25.000
What do we, how should we
respond to the monkeypox outbreak

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:28.110
or these sets of outbreaks, including,

00:27:28.110 --> 00:27:30.432
what can we learn from
our African colleagues

00:27:30.432 --> 00:27:32.473
who have been dealing this for many years?

00:27:32.473 --> 00:27:35.080
And by the way, so we
extended an invitation

00:27:35.080 --> 00:27:36.950
and there were some initial
interest from our colleagues

00:27:36.950 --> 00:27:40.690
from Africa CDC, but you
know, for various reasons,

00:27:40.690 --> 00:27:45.291
logistical reasons, they
couldn't join us for this panel.

00:27:45.291 --> 00:27:48.719
But, but what can be learned
from the previous experience

00:27:48.719 --> 00:27:53.414
in terms of addressing this
infection in these outbreaks

00:27:53.414 --> 00:27:56.461
and apply that to this
series of outbreaks.

00:27:56.461 --> 00:27:59.792
So I use the term outbreak
and outbreaks interchangeably.

00:27:59.792 --> 00:28:04.125
I do think it's a set of
outbreaks that are happening,

00:28:04.125 --> 00:28:08.572
that may have overlapping
factors that, you know,

00:28:08.572 --> 00:28:11.872
that has made this moment
where this is happening.

00:28:11.872 --> 00:28:16.210
But I think we may find out
that these are independent,

00:28:16.210 --> 00:28:18.862
but concurrent outbreaks.

00:28:18.862 --> 00:28:20.956
- Well, I think that what
you're saying is right.

00:28:20.956 --> 00:28:24.843
I mean, we're seeing a
series of global outbreaks

00:28:24.843 --> 00:28:27.203
and I think this goes back to, you know,

00:28:27.203 --> 00:28:31.275
that we're clearly seeing
rises in cases in Africa,

00:28:31.275 --> 00:28:34.876
in the traditionally endemic zone.

00:28:34.876 --> 00:28:37.708
And it's likely seeded
outbreaks that we just

00:28:37.708 --> 00:28:39.295
haven't been paying attention to.

00:28:39.295 --> 00:28:42.062
And in particular, because
the west African plague

00:28:42.062 --> 00:28:44.644
tends to have a more mild
presentation, all things

00:28:44.644 --> 00:28:49.184
being relative, and could be
mistaken for other things.

00:28:49.184 --> 00:28:53.186
And I think that it's one
of these things, if you're,

00:28:53.186 --> 00:28:55.544
you know, staring into the dark,
you might not see anything,

00:28:55.544 --> 00:28:58.254
you gotta turn the light,
shine a flashlight,

00:28:58.254 --> 00:28:59.394
you're gonna catch something.

00:28:59.394 --> 00:29:01.133
But if you turn all the lights on,

00:29:01.133 --> 00:29:02.265
you're gonna see a lot more.

00:29:02.265 --> 00:29:04.666
And I really think that this
is about situational awareness.

00:29:04.666 --> 00:29:07.833
We, I believe that some of these cases

00:29:10.894 --> 00:29:14.975
that we've seen in the
US are actually related,

00:29:14.975 --> 00:29:19.335
more closely related
genetically to the outbreak in,

00:29:19.335 --> 00:29:21.668
or the case in Texas, right?

00:29:22.543 --> 00:29:24.188
So that suggests that there have

00:29:24.188 --> 00:29:25.996
probably been multiple introductions.

00:29:25.996 --> 00:29:28.716
And now that we're looking for
it, we're actually seeing it.

00:29:28.716 --> 00:29:30.216
And this could have been
something that's been spreading

00:29:30.216 --> 00:29:33.165
for a while, even at a very low level,

00:29:33.165 --> 00:29:34.296
but then finally reached a network

00:29:34.296 --> 00:29:37.026
where it could propagate very quickly.

00:29:37.026 --> 00:29:38.575
And now we're seeing all these cases.

00:29:38.575 --> 00:29:41.391
So, your question I think
really is, "so what now?"

00:29:41.391 --> 00:29:45.786
What do we do now, how,
here we are, what do we do?

00:29:45.786 --> 00:29:47.449
And I think that there are a
number of things we need to do.

00:29:47.449 --> 00:29:49.396
I mean, first of all, we
need to be able to have

00:29:49.396 --> 00:29:50.826
this kind of situational awareness.

00:29:50.826 --> 00:29:51.659
So what do you need?

00:29:51.659 --> 00:29:54.397
You need good syndromic surveillance,

00:29:54.397 --> 00:29:57.064
you need good clinical pictures,

00:29:58.957 --> 00:30:02.106
and information so that
clinicians can actually make

00:30:02.106 --> 00:30:05.996
these diagnoses so that
people can identify themselves

00:30:05.996 --> 00:30:07.579
at particular risk.

00:30:08.533 --> 00:30:12.318
You need good risk
communication so that everybody

00:30:12.318 --> 00:30:16.091
understands what the risks
are, what, how this is all

00:30:16.091 --> 00:30:19.269
potentially going to impact them.

00:30:19.269 --> 00:30:21.170
Of course, beyond the clinical part,

00:30:21.170 --> 00:30:23.600
you need diagnostic capacity.

00:30:23.600 --> 00:30:25.652
And, right now we're very hampered

00:30:25.652 --> 00:30:27.748
by this diagnostic capacity.

00:30:27.748 --> 00:30:31.970
You know, I just saw a note
that there are several cases,

00:30:31.970 --> 00:30:34.661
I think 10 cases of
orthopox positive, you know,

00:30:34.661 --> 00:30:37.542
orthopox positive cases
in New York right now.

00:30:37.542 --> 00:30:39.111
So now they're gonna have to send them on

00:30:39.111 --> 00:30:40.440
for confirmation for monkeypox.

00:30:40.440 --> 00:30:42.492
And so this is a process,
you know, listen.

00:30:42.492 --> 00:30:45.440
At least we have orthopox
assays that are out there.

00:30:45.440 --> 00:30:49.092
And so we don't, you know,
that in and of itself,

00:30:49.092 --> 00:30:51.109
I think is gonna be relevant and then

00:30:51.109 --> 00:30:51.942
you can send it on for further,

00:30:51.942 --> 00:30:54.540
but we really are gonna have to expand

00:30:54.540 --> 00:30:58.040
our diagnostic capacity at a local level,

00:30:59.811 --> 00:31:01.512
at a national level, we're going to have

00:31:01.512 --> 00:31:04.561
to make this something
that's very accessible

00:31:04.561 --> 00:31:06.652
if we really wanna be
able to capture the cases.

00:31:06.652 --> 00:31:08.687
And, this is just standard public health.

00:31:08.687 --> 00:31:11.122
You're gonna need, ideally
you'd wanna have things

00:31:11.122 --> 00:31:13.738
like lateral flow assays and rapid

00:31:13.738 --> 00:31:16.222
or point of care diagnostics,
but even, you know,

00:31:16.222 --> 00:31:18.460
in the interim, really
rapidly scaling this up

00:31:18.460 --> 00:31:21.142
will be important, you know,
syndromic surveillance,

00:31:21.142 --> 00:31:23.771
communication, to everybody.

00:31:23.771 --> 00:31:25.690
And then we have to
have a really clear plan

00:31:25.690 --> 00:31:29.373
about prioritizing
vaccines and therapeutics,

00:31:29.373 --> 00:31:32.403
and who's gonna need them,
how they work for people,

00:31:32.403 --> 00:31:34.792
what the plan is going to be to do this.

00:31:34.792 --> 00:31:36.391
And that really means that we need to,

00:31:36.391 --> 00:31:38.440
to be scaling that up as well.

00:31:38.440 --> 00:31:39.683
Now, at the same time,

00:31:39.683 --> 00:31:43.061
we have to really be scaling
up all of our research.

00:31:43.061 --> 00:31:44.858
You know, to be able to truly
understand what's going on.

00:31:44.858 --> 00:31:46.321
We have to be able to characterize

00:31:46.321 --> 00:31:48.539
transmission mechanisms and its potential,

00:31:48.539 --> 00:31:50.589
we need to characterize
clinical presentation

00:31:50.589 --> 00:31:54.340
and disease severity,
and various risk groups.

00:31:54.340 --> 00:31:55.603
We need to know what the virus does

00:31:55.603 --> 00:31:58.456
in immunocompromised
individuals, we need to know,

00:31:58.456 --> 00:32:00.499
is there an asymptomatic
form of infection?

00:32:00.499 --> 00:32:01.933
Do asymptomatic people transmit?

00:32:01.933 --> 00:32:04.729
I mean, we've done this dance before.

00:32:04.729 --> 00:32:06.081
We know all the things that we don't know.

00:32:06.081 --> 00:32:07.660
And I think the most important
thing is to be humble

00:32:07.660 --> 00:32:09.581
about what we know and what we don't know,

00:32:09.581 --> 00:32:10.940
because what we know is really about

00:32:10.940 --> 00:32:12.841
how this virus has been
spreading traditionally

00:32:12.841 --> 00:32:16.499
in a very different epidemiologic
and ecological landscape.

00:32:16.499 --> 00:32:20.020
So I think that the key is,
you know, saying, "okay,

00:32:20.020 --> 00:32:22.590
well, here's what we know so far."

00:32:22.590 --> 00:32:23.899
Now we need to go further so that

00:32:23.899 --> 00:32:26.211
we truly do understand that,
so we can get in front of it

00:32:26.211 --> 00:32:28.851
because stakes are frankly very high.

00:32:28.851 --> 00:32:31.141
You know, if this virus
becomes, as I said before,

00:32:31.141 --> 00:32:33.278
if it becomes established
even at a very low level

00:32:33.278 --> 00:32:36.662
in human populations, or we see
reverse spillover, you know,

00:32:36.662 --> 00:32:39.745
we're really gonna have to reconsider

00:32:42.693 --> 00:32:47.640
how we view a circulating
orthopoxvirus in humans.

00:32:47.640 --> 00:32:49.350
And do we think that this is
enough of a risk that we're

00:32:49.350 --> 00:32:53.789
going to have to really
reconsider what vaccination means?

00:32:53.789 --> 00:32:54.762
And that was one of the big benefits of

00:32:54.762 --> 00:32:56.381
smallpox eradication program, right?

00:32:56.381 --> 00:32:59.333
You eradicate a virus, you're able to

00:32:59.333 --> 00:33:02.323
prevent the human suffering,
which is the key issue,

00:33:02.323 --> 00:33:05.589
but then you're also able to
dedicate those resources for

00:33:05.589 --> 00:33:09.581
manufacturer of vaccines and
distribution of vaccines to

00:33:09.581 --> 00:33:13.664
other virus, or other
pathogens, to other issues.

00:33:14.591 --> 00:33:18.002
And so, you know, it's an
enormous step backwards

00:33:18.002 --> 00:33:20.779
for public health to
have to really reconsider

00:33:20.779 --> 00:33:25.779
the utilization of smallpox
vaccine again, in public health.

00:33:26.082 --> 00:33:29.249
So the stakes are quite high, I think.

00:33:31.762 --> 00:33:33.002
You're muted.

00:33:33.002 --> 00:33:35.768
- Yeah, so are you absolutely right.

00:33:35.768 --> 00:33:37.739
And, and that brings me to Nate,

00:33:37.739 --> 00:33:41.446
what can we do to prepare for
a local cluster if it happens,

00:33:41.446 --> 00:33:44.228
what research questions
do you plan to address?

00:33:44.228 --> 00:33:46.426
I know on a, in your, in an ideal world,

00:33:46.426 --> 00:33:49.230
you would still be, we
wouldn't have had a pandemic.

00:33:49.230 --> 00:33:50.920
We wouldn't have had a monkeypox outbreak

00:33:50.920 --> 00:33:55.360
and you would be working
on dengue, but, you know,

00:33:55.360 --> 00:33:58.977
we all know that this will
require a scientific response.

00:33:58.977 --> 00:34:01.058
And so what do you
think, what should we do

00:34:01.058 --> 00:34:04.007
with the local cluster and
what questions we should ask?

00:34:04.007 --> 00:34:05.299
Especially what do you think,

00:34:05.299 --> 00:34:07.618
you know, what you are planning to ask.

00:34:07.618 --> 00:34:10.549
- Yeah, so, first I wanna start by

00:34:10.549 --> 00:34:12.320
just reiterating some points from Anne

00:34:12.320 --> 00:34:14.650
that I think are
incredibly important here,

00:34:14.650 --> 00:34:17.998
is that we first need a
better handle on the extent

00:34:17.998 --> 00:34:21.528
of local transmission,
if it's happening, right.

00:34:21.528 --> 00:34:25.509
Like in our communities,
it's, really feels like

00:34:25.509 --> 00:34:27.547
we're getting like the
tip of the iceberg here.

00:34:27.547 --> 00:34:29.920
And, I'm not saying it's a COVID situation

00:34:29.920 --> 00:34:32.330
where there's like, you know, gonna have,

00:34:32.330 --> 00:34:34.771
hundreds of thousands of
people being in infected

00:34:34.771 --> 00:34:38.309
in just a short amount
of time, but it's likely

00:34:38.309 --> 00:34:40.718
a lot more than what we know.

00:34:40.718 --> 00:34:43.629
And this absolutely needs to include

00:34:43.629 --> 00:34:46.029
the rollout of frontline diagnostics.

00:34:46.029 --> 00:34:49.779
So currently the testing
is still encompassed

00:34:50.736 --> 00:34:54.830
in the CDC's Laboratory Response Network.

00:34:54.830 --> 00:34:58.998
So this includes more than 60
labs across all 50 US states.

00:34:58.998 --> 00:35:02.885
And most of these are
located in state facilities.

00:35:02.885 --> 00:35:07.119
So if you were to present
to the ED or clinician

00:35:07.119 --> 00:35:09.426
with monkeypox-like symptoms,

00:35:09.426 --> 00:35:11.336
what they would do is
they would take a swab

00:35:11.336 --> 00:35:14.047
of the pox or lesion, and
they would first send it to

00:35:14.047 --> 00:35:18.964
the state labs for this broad,
you know, family-level test.

00:35:20.122 --> 00:35:21.255
And then if it's positive there,

00:35:21.255 --> 00:35:23.927
then the samples are sent
to the CDC for confirmation.

00:35:23.927 --> 00:35:26.454
They do a genotyping assay
and chart, I mean if it's,

00:35:26.454 --> 00:35:28.885
you know, the west African
strain as they have been doing,

00:35:28.885 --> 00:35:30.064
and then they do sequencing.

00:35:30.064 --> 00:35:32.743
And this is the similar sort
of setup that was, that we had

00:35:32.743 --> 00:35:36.252
at the beginning of
SARS-CoV-2 in early 2020.

00:35:36.252 --> 00:35:39.424
This system works great
for rare pathogens,

00:35:39.424 --> 00:35:41.302
because hospitals and diagnostic labs

00:35:41.302 --> 00:35:43.770
cannot reasonably be
expected to have tests

00:35:43.770 --> 00:35:47.102
for every single pathogen
that is out there, right?

00:35:47.102 --> 00:35:49.914
This is why the Laboratory
Response Network exists,

00:35:49.914 --> 00:35:51.934
so that when we have something
strange that comes up,

00:35:51.934 --> 00:35:53.165
we can figure out what it is,

00:35:53.165 --> 00:35:56.165
and the CDC can respond around that.

00:35:57.115 --> 00:35:59.582
However, given that monkeypox is here,

00:35:59.582 --> 00:36:00.572
like we know that it's here,

00:36:00.572 --> 00:36:01.683
there are cases that are rising,

00:36:01.683 --> 00:36:03.164
they're rising around the country,

00:36:03.164 --> 00:36:03.997
or in around the world.

00:36:03.997 --> 00:36:05.973
It's my opinion that we
really need to roll out

00:36:05.973 --> 00:36:08.234
these frontline tests,
and to be able to reduce

00:36:08.234 --> 00:36:10.893
both the resulting times
of having tests to go from

00:36:10.893 --> 00:36:13.610
sent to a lab and then
sent to another lab,

00:36:13.610 --> 00:36:15.845
and also to increase testing capacity.

00:36:15.845 --> 00:36:17.133
But maybe even more importantly,

00:36:17.133 --> 00:36:19.724
it might make ordering these tests easier,

00:36:19.724 --> 00:36:22.073
especially in the
circumstances when the symptoms

00:36:22.073 --> 00:36:24.805
resemble so many other common diseases.

00:36:24.805 --> 00:36:27.661
We're hearing of a lot of
cases in MSM communities

00:36:27.661 --> 00:36:30.633
where these symptoms look like other STIs.

00:36:30.633 --> 00:36:32.783
There might be just one or two pox

00:36:32.783 --> 00:36:35.172
that are located on or near the genitals,

00:36:35.172 --> 00:36:39.686
and if someone is not astute,
one, it could be missed.

00:36:39.686 --> 00:36:42.394
But also just could think
it might be something else.

00:36:42.394 --> 00:36:44.502
And we almost need to
include that right now,

00:36:44.502 --> 00:36:46.483
in general STI testing.

00:36:46.483 --> 00:36:48.724
And that can't necessarily
happen, I think,

00:36:48.724 --> 00:36:51.641
in the current system that we have.

00:36:53.264 --> 00:36:57.092
But, it's not really clear
to me what the frontline labs

00:36:57.092 --> 00:36:59.893
would actually use, because there's an,

00:36:59.893 --> 00:37:03.572
I don't know of any commercially
available monkeypox test

00:37:03.572 --> 00:37:05.364
that is approved by the FDA,

00:37:05.364 --> 00:37:08.032
that we could just put in all these labs.

00:37:08.032 --> 00:37:09.672
That is a problem.

00:37:09.672 --> 00:37:12.644
So my group's first goal,
just kind of how we did,

00:37:12.644 --> 00:37:14.263
I feel like deja vu a bit.

00:37:14.263 --> 00:37:15.633
I know this is not COVID,

00:37:15.633 --> 00:37:17.713
but there are some things
that are related to this.

00:37:17.713 --> 00:37:21.464
And at the beginning
of the COVID pandemic,

00:37:21.464 --> 00:37:23.673
our initial response was like,

00:37:23.673 --> 00:37:26.874
how do we help diagnostics
in any way possible?

00:37:26.874 --> 00:37:29.333
So what we are doing is that
we are just working with

00:37:29.333 --> 00:37:31.414
several different PCR tests to optimize

00:37:31.414 --> 00:37:34.325
the different conditions,
to do comparisons,

00:37:34.325 --> 00:37:37.222
to allow different diagnostic labs

00:37:37.222 --> 00:37:38.708
to be able to look at results,

00:37:38.708 --> 00:37:42.736
select what tests they wanna
do for internal validation,

00:37:42.736 --> 00:37:47.486
until we actually have some
commercial options available.

00:37:48.594 --> 00:37:51.307
Relatedly, we're also
working to streamline

00:37:51.307 --> 00:37:52.495
the sequencing assays.

00:37:52.495 --> 00:37:55.830
Essentially our goal is
to take COVIDSeq, right?

00:37:55.830 --> 00:37:58.543
All this 11 million sequences
that have been generated

00:37:58.543 --> 00:38:00.445
for SARS-CoV-2 sequencing,

00:38:00.445 --> 00:38:02.999
and adapt monkeypox virus sequencing

00:38:02.999 --> 00:38:04.064
to fit in those systems.

00:38:04.064 --> 00:38:05.580
'Cause we know labs know how to do it,

00:38:05.580 --> 00:38:08.235
there's a lot of capacity that's built up

00:38:08.235 --> 00:38:09.973
and we need to build to validate that.

00:38:09.973 --> 00:38:11.942
So we can have, along
with the diagnostics,

00:38:11.942 --> 00:38:13.872
we can have the sequencing,
'cause we know how important

00:38:13.872 --> 00:38:16.333
the sequencing is now, I think.

00:38:16.333 --> 00:38:19.256
And then finally, if we
have a local cluster, right,

00:38:19.256 --> 00:38:22.913
it's our plan to conduct
clinical studies to determine

00:38:22.913 --> 00:38:25.542
first, like when and where
do you find the virus

00:38:25.542 --> 00:38:28.924
in various parts of the body,
different tissues, you know,

00:38:28.924 --> 00:38:30.056
sample types and everything.

00:38:30.056 --> 00:38:33.815
And both, this could help
improve diagnostics, right?

00:38:33.815 --> 00:38:35.751
If we could, if for example,

00:38:35.751 --> 00:38:39.031
if there's asymptomatic
transmission that is happening,

00:38:39.031 --> 00:38:41.553
if the primary form of being able to test

00:38:41.553 --> 00:38:45.962
for monkeypox virus is via
a lesion or pox on the skin,

00:38:45.962 --> 00:38:49.404
and if you don't have one,
how do we test for it, right?

00:38:49.404 --> 00:38:53.202
If we can detect this in
oral fluids or saliva,

00:38:53.202 --> 00:38:55.662
and if that could be a great
test, that would be fantastic.

00:38:55.662 --> 00:38:57.685
We would like to be able to
know that so we could roll

00:38:57.685 --> 00:38:59.303
something like that out.

00:38:59.303 --> 00:39:00.694
And also I think that this would help

00:39:00.694 --> 00:39:02.503
shed light on transmission.

00:39:02.503 --> 00:39:04.782
If we don't necessarily
know how it's happening,

00:39:04.782 --> 00:39:06.642
if there are cases where
transmission is happening,

00:39:06.642 --> 00:39:09.093
where it's not very
intimate, close, you know,

00:39:09.093 --> 00:39:11.122
skin to skin contact, right,

00:39:11.122 --> 00:39:12.208
we need to be able to use that,

00:39:12.208 --> 00:39:15.698
so we can implement the
public health practices

00:39:15.698 --> 00:39:19.300
that we know that can
then stop transmission.

00:39:19.300 --> 00:39:20.454
- Yeah.

00:39:20.454 --> 00:39:21.808
So the only way I know

00:39:21.808 --> 00:39:24.196
of controlling an outbreak
is to get ahead of it,

00:39:24.196 --> 00:39:26.716
and we are still following
this, this series of outbreaks.

00:39:26.716 --> 00:39:28.617
As long as you're behind an outbreak,

00:39:28.617 --> 00:39:30.068
you have little chance of controlling it.

00:39:30.068 --> 00:39:32.773
But again, just to caution
everyone that this is now,

00:39:32.773 --> 00:39:35.014
we are not looking at the start

00:39:35.014 --> 00:39:37.431
of another COVID-19 pandemic,

00:39:38.677 --> 00:39:42.053
but things can be significantly impactful

00:39:42.053 --> 00:39:44.970
even without getting to that level.

00:39:46.450 --> 00:39:49.344
I hope that doesn't
become our only benchmark

00:39:49.344 --> 00:39:52.304
of paying attention to
these kinds of diseases.

00:39:52.304 --> 00:39:56.471
Akiko, you know, obviously
like any disaster movie

00:39:58.115 --> 00:40:02.193
included in an outbreak,
vaccines are the heroes.

00:40:02.193 --> 00:40:05.723
And the narrative arc ends
that with an effective vaccine.

00:40:05.723 --> 00:40:06.880
We know in real life,

00:40:06.880 --> 00:40:09.841
the story is a little
bit more complicated.

00:40:09.841 --> 00:40:13.475
What do you think about
the pipeline for monkeypox,

00:40:13.475 --> 00:40:16.733
the vaccine pipeline for monkeypox,

00:40:16.733 --> 00:40:18.816
and any thoughts on that?

00:40:19.734 --> 00:40:21.314
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:40:21.314 --> 00:40:25.010
So I'm glad that the
immunology is the hero (laughs)

00:40:25.010 --> 00:40:26.725
in most of these situations.

00:40:26.725 --> 00:40:28.385
- Well, I would say more
broadly vaccinology,

00:40:28.385 --> 00:40:31.706
as a vaccinologist, I think, you know,

00:40:31.706 --> 00:40:34.911
immunology is part of that
family of disciplines.

00:40:34.911 --> 00:40:36.016
- Absolutely.

00:40:36.016 --> 00:40:39.333
So yeah, the good news is
that there are vaccines

00:40:39.333 --> 00:40:41.754
that are effective, as
I already discussed,

00:40:41.754 --> 00:40:44.575
against the monkeypox, and there is one

00:40:44.575 --> 00:40:49.081
that is currently FDA
approved called JYNNEOS.

00:40:49.081 --> 00:40:51.312
it's also known as in Imvanex,

00:40:51.312 --> 00:40:54.584
which is a replication-defective vaccine

00:40:54.584 --> 00:40:56.946
that's given subcutaneously.

00:40:56.946 --> 00:40:59.834
And it's a live vaccine,
produced from the strain

00:40:59.834 --> 00:41:02.324
called "modified vaccinia Ankara."

00:41:02.324 --> 00:41:03.723
And it's an, like I said,

00:41:03.723 --> 00:41:07.444
it's an attenuated and
non-replicating virus,

00:41:07.444 --> 00:41:10.072
vaccine, that can be given to people.

00:41:10.072 --> 00:41:12.739
And that is already FDA approved

00:41:13.632 --> 00:41:16.674
to prevent monkeypox disease.

00:41:16.674 --> 00:41:20.774
But the strategic national stockpile has

00:41:20.774 --> 00:41:23.864
other smallpox vaccines
that could also be deployed

00:41:23.864 --> 00:41:27.114
if needed, such as the more traditional

00:41:28.316 --> 00:41:31.566
vaccinia virus vaccine called ACAM2000.

00:41:32.495 --> 00:41:34.867
It's also known as IMVAMUNE,

00:41:34.867 --> 00:41:37.218
and it's a live vaccinia vaccine

00:41:37.218 --> 00:41:39.855
that's given percutaneously.

00:41:39.855 --> 00:41:42.107
That's sort of the, closely related to

00:41:42.107 --> 00:41:46.034
the original vaccine
that was used in the 70s.

00:41:46.034 --> 00:41:49.429
But this one is produced
in the laboratory setting,

00:41:49.429 --> 00:41:53.056
and tissue culture,
purified vaccinia virus

00:41:53.056 --> 00:41:55.416
that's been given to people for

00:41:55.416 --> 00:41:58.737
prevention of smallpox disease,

00:41:58.737 --> 00:42:00.754
and I think could be applicable

00:42:00.754 --> 00:42:03.171
to monkeypox disease as well.

00:42:04.005 --> 00:42:08.724
And there's one more vaccine
that is in the stockpile,

00:42:08.724 --> 00:42:12.257
which is the Aventis
Pasteur Smallpox Vaccine,

00:42:12.257 --> 00:42:14.674
which is still an investigational vaccine,

00:42:14.674 --> 00:42:18.976
but that can be used under
emergency use authorization.

00:42:18.976 --> 00:42:21.876
So there are these vaccines that are known

00:42:21.876 --> 00:42:26.849
to work against smallpox, that
could be used for monkeypox.

00:42:26.849 --> 00:42:29.188
And as I said, there's only,

00:42:29.188 --> 00:42:33.902
already one that's FDA
approved for the monkeypox.

00:42:33.902 --> 00:42:37.643
And then I know that other
companies like Moderna

00:42:37.643 --> 00:42:42.643
are already working on
developing a monkeypox vaccine.

00:42:42.663 --> 00:42:46.259
So hopefully there'll be
plenty of different vaccines

00:42:46.259 --> 00:42:50.454
in the pipeline that can
combat the diseases if needed.

00:42:50.454 --> 00:42:53.617
- So, having been involved
with, in the early 2000s,

00:42:53.617 --> 00:42:56.476
in some sort of planning around a possible

00:42:56.476 --> 00:42:59.138
smallpox bio-terrorist attack,
there was a lot of discussion

00:42:59.138 --> 00:43:02.716
of that, you know, after 9/11.

00:43:02.716 --> 00:43:04.315
And I had the privilege of being involved

00:43:04.315 --> 00:43:08.286
with some vaccine policy
relations, especially safety.

00:43:08.286 --> 00:43:12.066
One of our initial thinking
was that people like me

00:43:12.066 --> 00:43:16.796
who have smallpox scars
could be on the front line

00:43:16.796 --> 00:43:21.524
of the response, because
of that kind of immunity.

00:43:21.524 --> 00:43:24.455
And here, obviously cross
protection against monkeypox,

00:43:24.455 --> 00:43:26.896
as you alluded to, that
we have evidence of that,

00:43:26.896 --> 00:43:29.585
but we need to do updated studies.

00:43:29.585 --> 00:43:31.006
Do you think that could
be a part of strategies?

00:43:31.006 --> 00:43:34.416
So people, one part of
diversity in this country

00:43:34.416 --> 00:43:36.187
is that there are people who are older,

00:43:36.187 --> 00:43:38.867
who have had smallpox
vaccine, and people who are

00:43:38.867 --> 00:43:41.362
relatively younger,
because they're immigrants,

00:43:41.362 --> 00:43:43.599
have received a smallpox vaccine,

00:43:43.599 --> 00:43:45.788
and this is true for the
rest of the country as well.

00:43:45.788 --> 00:43:48.017
Do you think there is a value in doing

00:43:48.017 --> 00:43:52.184
those kinds of studies of,
you know, the residual,

00:43:53.230 --> 00:43:57.569
or ongoing cross-protection,
to make sure that we have

00:43:57.569 --> 00:44:00.149
a sort of a more comprehensive
public health response,

00:44:00.149 --> 00:44:02.958
where we leverage that diversity?

00:44:02.958 --> 00:44:06.148
- Yeah, I think that is
really an important issue,

00:44:06.148 --> 00:44:08.909
especially for those who may
be coming in close contact

00:44:08.909 --> 00:44:12.237
with patients with the
monkeypox infection.

00:44:12.237 --> 00:44:14.218
And even though I said that, you know,

00:44:14.218 --> 00:44:18.051
pox viruses provide
robust, lifelong immunity,

00:44:18.926 --> 00:44:22.898
this is just an assumption
against the monkeypox right now.

00:44:22.898 --> 00:44:23.731
- [Saad] Yeah.

00:44:23.731 --> 00:44:25.401
- So we do need to do much better,

00:44:25.401 --> 00:44:30.143
sort of zero surveillance,
and if possible, T-cell assays

00:44:30.143 --> 00:44:32.988
to see, like, how long
does the immunity last,

00:44:32.988 --> 00:44:36.807
like against the monkeypox,
and whether we need a booster.

00:44:36.807 --> 00:44:38.546
And of course a booster is always

00:44:38.546 --> 00:44:43.115
a great thing to do if there's
sufficient vaccine available,

00:44:43.115 --> 00:44:46.476
but as Anne alluded to, the primary, like,

00:44:46.476 --> 00:44:49.268
the most urgent target population, may be

00:44:49.268 --> 00:44:52.757
the people who are living
in the endemic area.

00:44:52.757 --> 00:44:54.914
So, you know, again, how do we distribute

00:44:54.914 --> 00:44:58.727
the vaccine dose in a
proper and equitable manner

00:44:58.727 --> 00:45:00.242
to control this pandemic?

00:45:00.242 --> 00:45:04.268
That's sort of, yeah, an
important question going forward.

00:45:04.268 --> 00:45:07.485
- Hey, I'm gonna sort of frame a question

00:45:07.485 --> 00:45:10.133
from Elizabeth Tuckwell who
has posed that question,

00:45:10.133 --> 00:45:12.391
I think the intention
is to clarify something.

00:45:12.391 --> 00:45:13.494
I know the answer to that,

00:45:13.494 --> 00:45:17.706
but I think we should address
that question head on,

00:45:17.706 --> 00:45:20.706
early on, which is, is there any lab

00:45:21.851 --> 00:45:24.686
that might have enhanced monkeypox,

00:45:24.686 --> 00:45:28.155
such as some speculated for COVID?

00:45:28.155 --> 00:45:32.236
I think we should address that
head on so that there isn't

00:45:32.236 --> 00:45:37.025
an information gap where a lot
of conspiracies then evolve.

00:45:37.025 --> 00:45:39.972
Nate, I'll put you on the spot for that.

00:45:39.972 --> 00:45:43.076
- I, so I don't think there's
any evidence for that.

00:45:43.076 --> 00:45:47.154
I mean, and there's really
is, Anne had mentioned too,

00:45:47.154 --> 00:45:49.566
there's not really a lot of
labs that are even working

00:45:49.566 --> 00:45:52.816
on monkeypox for this to happen, but I,

00:45:53.848 --> 00:45:57.712
there's nothing that concerning that,

00:45:57.712 --> 00:46:00.679
any sort of red flags that I have seen,

00:46:00.679 --> 00:46:03.393
that would take me down
this route, but you know.

00:46:03.393 --> 00:46:07.226
Of course there's, and
also specifically here

00:46:08.412 --> 00:46:10.743
with the viruses from this outbreak,

00:46:10.743 --> 00:46:15.134
we have a recent common
ancestor from the outbreak

00:46:15.134 --> 00:46:16.717
in Nigeria in 2017,

00:46:17.602 --> 00:46:19.408
and then the evolutionary
patterns that we've seen

00:46:19.408 --> 00:46:21.012
since then make sense biologically

00:46:21.012 --> 00:46:23.781
with human-to-human transmission.

00:46:23.781 --> 00:46:25.313
So if there was any engineering,

00:46:25.313 --> 00:46:28.468
it wouldn't even be
clear what that would be,

00:46:28.468 --> 00:46:30.851
that would've aided anything here.

00:46:30.851 --> 00:46:35.124
So that, I think there would
be a very remote possibility.

00:46:35.124 --> 00:46:36.131
- Yeah, and then the other things is

00:46:36.131 --> 00:46:40.084
that based, just the
probability of the fact,

00:46:40.084 --> 00:46:42.772
because there is a natural, you know,

00:46:42.772 --> 00:46:45.172
the natural transmission
is happening for a while

00:46:45.172 --> 00:46:48.255
in a pretty substantial endemic area.

00:46:49.151 --> 00:46:50.899
So again, if you, if you look at

00:46:50.899 --> 00:46:53.281
that kind of a contrafactual,

00:46:53.281 --> 00:46:55.932
it is more likely, much more likely for it

00:46:55.932 --> 00:46:57.203
to have come from a, quote unquote,

00:46:57.203 --> 00:46:59.870
"natural source," at this point.

00:47:01.060 --> 00:47:02.102
- I wanna just,

00:47:02.102 --> 00:47:03.123
reply to that.
- [Saad] Yeah, go ahead.

00:47:03.123 --> 00:47:04.311
Please go ahead.
- A little bit.

00:47:04.311 --> 00:47:07.063
Because, I mean, listen,
we've all been, I mean,

00:47:07.063 --> 00:47:08.543
if you look back at even
the papers that we wrote

00:47:08.543 --> 00:47:11.382
back in 2010, not just me, I mean,

00:47:11.382 --> 00:47:14.612
our colleagues at the CDC, all of us

00:47:14.612 --> 00:47:15.825
were saying, listen, you gotta,

00:47:15.825 --> 00:47:20.240
you've gotta be able to
control monkeypox now,

00:47:20.240 --> 00:47:22.610
and really understand it, because,

00:47:22.610 --> 00:47:26.391
if we see cases continue to
rise, as we predict they will,

00:47:26.391 --> 00:47:29.261
we're eventually gonna see importations.

00:47:29.261 --> 00:47:33.761
And that's just, I mean,
this isn't a mysterious virus

00:47:34.692 --> 00:47:39.692
that we, you know, that we
hadn't identified before.

00:47:39.852 --> 00:47:42.685
This is a known pathogen, that did

00:47:44.601 --> 00:47:48.124
exactly what we predicted
it could potentially do,

00:47:48.124 --> 00:47:50.520
which is if it happened to get itself

00:47:50.520 --> 00:47:54.652
into an appropriate network,
an advantageous network

00:47:54.652 --> 00:47:57.363
that it would be able to spread rapidly.

00:47:57.363 --> 00:47:58.196
And that's what we're seeing.

00:47:58.196 --> 00:48:00.280
Now of course we have all of
these, you know, concerns.

00:48:00.280 --> 00:48:02.370
Okay, well, what happens
if it starts getting

00:48:02.370 --> 00:48:03.891
into immunocompromised populations?

00:48:03.891 --> 00:48:05.980
Could we see it mutate more?

00:48:05.980 --> 00:48:09.063
If we see, you know, this kind of ability

00:48:09.063 --> 00:48:10.523
to continue to spread, I mean,

00:48:10.523 --> 00:48:13.692
we're gonna get given
this virus some runway

00:48:13.692 --> 00:48:16.191
that it hasn't had before.

00:48:16.191 --> 00:48:18.833
But this isn't a novel
pathogen that we've never seen

00:48:18.833 --> 00:48:23.610
before, and it's doing exactly
what we expect it would do,

00:48:23.610 --> 00:48:26.699
having gotten into the
networks that it's gotten into.

00:48:26.699 --> 00:48:28.100
I mean, there's, it just,

00:48:28.100 --> 00:48:30.852
the clinical presentation
is a little bit different

00:48:30.852 --> 00:48:32.724
than we had been paying attention to,

00:48:32.724 --> 00:48:35.438
but it turns out there have
been records of this in,

00:48:35.438 --> 00:48:37.518
I mean, reports of this in Nigeria,

00:48:37.518 --> 00:48:39.079
that just nobody had
the funds or the ability

00:48:39.079 --> 00:48:41.709
to do the kind of research
to be able to document this

00:48:41.709 --> 00:48:43.343
where it was already happening.

00:48:43.343 --> 00:48:47.642
Now, we're seeing it happen,
on a much larger global scale.

00:48:47.642 --> 00:48:49.731
And I'm sure our African
colleagues would say, "hey, well,

00:48:49.731 --> 00:48:51.418
you know, we've been
seeing this for a while.

00:48:51.418 --> 00:48:54.800
If you'd invested in our
networks and our ability

00:48:54.800 --> 00:48:57.878
to control this in a
place where it is endemic,

00:48:57.878 --> 00:48:59.278
then we'd already know about this,

00:48:59.278 --> 00:49:00.988
and we'd already know what the risks are."

00:49:00.988 --> 00:49:03.571
And we just haven't done that.

00:49:03.571 --> 00:49:06.154
We've ignored that possibility.

00:49:08.029 --> 00:49:11.199
And of course, this is a
situation where, you know,

00:49:11.199 --> 00:49:14.960
we've come into this,
I keep likening this,

00:49:14.960 --> 00:49:15.799
it's the same as always.

00:49:15.799 --> 00:49:17.451
My analogy for this is like,

00:49:17.451 --> 00:49:19.279
we've tuned into a new TV series

00:49:19.279 --> 00:49:21.320
and we don't know what
episode we've landed on.

00:49:21.320 --> 00:49:23.247
Was it two, was it five, is it ten?

00:49:23.247 --> 00:49:24.777
Are we in season three?

00:49:24.777 --> 00:49:26.257
You know, where are we,
I don't know, I mean,

00:49:26.257 --> 00:49:28.255
but we definitely missed some
of the origin story here.

00:49:28.255 --> 00:49:30.668
But the early origin story, we've got.

00:49:30.668 --> 00:49:32.940
Like, we know how monkeypox,
where monkeypox came from.

00:49:32.940 --> 00:49:36.031
We know that this was
always a possibility.

00:49:36.031 --> 00:49:38.638
It was just low on the list of priorities,

00:49:38.638 --> 00:49:41.339
of something to be able
to pay attention to.

00:49:41.339 --> 00:49:43.261
And even we knew there was
this existential threat,

00:49:43.261 --> 00:49:44.489
that this could possibly happen, I mean,

00:49:44.489 --> 00:49:47.754
it's always like, the squeaky
wheel gets the grease,

00:49:47.754 --> 00:49:50.552
and monkeypox was not squeaky enough

00:49:50.552 --> 00:49:52.802
to get that kind of grease.

00:49:54.238 --> 00:49:56.808
And now we are here, in a situation where

00:49:56.808 --> 00:50:00.336
we've given this virus a,
you know, an opportunity

00:50:00.336 --> 00:50:04.489
to spread, it spreads
well with close contact,

00:50:04.489 --> 00:50:08.989
and fomites, and respiratory
droplets, at the minimum,

00:50:09.912 --> 00:50:10.881
those are the things we know.

00:50:10.881 --> 00:50:12.410
And we, you know, hey.

00:50:12.410 --> 00:50:16.021
Like, what could be better
than sexual networks for that?

00:50:16.021 --> 00:50:18.488
That just, it just makes sense, you know.

00:50:18.488 --> 00:50:20.958
I think at the end of the day, so...

00:50:20.958 --> 00:50:21.809
- And now, so again, actually,

00:50:21.809 --> 00:50:25.071
I'll take the liberty to
put you on the spot, Anne.

00:50:25.071 --> 00:50:26.877
In terms of, because
we have a lot of people

00:50:26.877 --> 00:50:29.730
who are early stage
investigators, and students,

00:50:29.730 --> 00:50:31.730
who attend these events.

00:50:32.743 --> 00:50:35.190
So tell me a little bit
about your experience

00:50:35.190 --> 00:50:38.940
with getting resources
for monkeypox and how,

00:50:40.002 --> 00:50:42.623
and your case study, which to my mind

00:50:42.623 --> 00:50:45.456
is a microcosm of how governments

00:50:46.434 --> 00:50:48.159
and international entities
should invest better

00:50:48.159 --> 00:50:49.230
in this kind of work.

00:50:49.230 --> 00:50:53.411
Because your K Award, which
is a primary investigator,

00:50:53.411 --> 00:50:56.421
early stage investigator
award, was on monkeypox.

00:50:56.421 --> 00:51:00.450
So you know, would you care
to elaborate a little bit

00:51:00.450 --> 00:51:02.421
on what you had to face?

00:51:02.421 --> 00:51:05.010
- Sure, so, you know, I came to this

00:51:05.010 --> 00:51:09.250
from our common ancestor,
our common advisor,

00:51:09.250 --> 00:51:11.932
Mark Steinhoff at Hopkins.

00:51:11.932 --> 00:51:14.660
And I went to NIH, actually.

00:51:14.660 --> 00:51:17.327
So I was at the center at NICHD.

00:51:19.746 --> 00:51:23.382
And I was a program officer
on a project in DRC.

00:51:23.382 --> 00:51:25.769
And when I was there,

00:51:25.769 --> 00:51:27.998
I recognized that there were
cases of monkeypox there.

00:51:27.998 --> 00:51:30.491
And because I'd worked
in disease eradication

00:51:30.491 --> 00:51:31.541
as a Peace Corps volunteer,

00:51:31.541 --> 00:51:33.753
and then I worked in polio eradication.

00:51:33.753 --> 00:51:35.881
I worked with Mark on
pediatric infectious diseases,

00:51:35.881 --> 00:51:37.610
vaccine preventable diseases.

00:51:37.610 --> 00:51:38.639
You know, I was really attuned

00:51:38.639 --> 00:51:40.641
to this issue of vaccine
preventable diseases.

00:51:40.641 --> 00:51:45.560
And when I met with my now
very long term collaborators

00:51:45.560 --> 00:51:47.993
and close friends, Jean-Jacques Muyembe

00:51:47.993 --> 00:51:50.629
and also the late Emile Okitolanda,

00:51:50.629 --> 00:51:52.422
the Dean of the Kinshasa
School of Public Health,

00:51:52.422 --> 00:51:54.309
director of National Lab, Muyembe.

00:51:54.309 --> 00:51:57.042
They mentioned that there were
all these cases of monkeypox

00:51:57.042 --> 00:51:58.029
that weren't being recorded.

00:51:58.029 --> 00:51:59.548
And I thought that this was
really important and interesting

00:51:59.548 --> 00:52:01.213
but at the time, you know,

00:52:01.213 --> 00:52:02.821
there really wasn't a mechanism
to be able to send them

00:52:02.821 --> 00:52:05.819
to get tested, and the Congo
didn't have that ability.

00:52:05.819 --> 00:52:10.819
So we sent them to the, you
know, just using, I don't,

00:52:11.110 --> 00:52:13.372
like we scrounged around for some funds

00:52:13.372 --> 00:52:16.530
and were able to send them to Germany

00:52:16.530 --> 00:52:19.310
for the pox coordinating lab that was

00:52:19.310 --> 00:52:20.946
able to do these analyses.

00:52:20.946 --> 00:52:22.117
And we were able, it was our first paper

00:52:22.117 --> 00:52:23.926
that was in EID, just really demonstrating

00:52:23.926 --> 00:52:26.387
that we were seeing
increasing cases of monkeypox.

00:52:26.387 --> 00:52:29.417
So we were able then to
leverage that, to get a grant,

00:52:29.417 --> 00:52:31.386
it was, you know, it was at the time,

00:52:31.386 --> 00:52:34.778
it was a sizeable grant
to be able to look at

00:52:34.778 --> 00:52:36.686
the increase in cases of monkeypox.

00:52:36.686 --> 00:52:38.685
And so we set up enhanced
disease surveillance,

00:52:38.685 --> 00:52:41.275
and some of the early sites,
I mean, it wasn't perfect.

00:52:41.275 --> 00:52:43.455
We did the best we could,
we did some serial surveys.

00:52:43.455 --> 00:52:47.086
And we really found this
increasing rise in cases.

00:52:47.086 --> 00:52:49.167
Meanwhile, I got my K on monkeypox,

00:52:49.167 --> 00:52:51.135
epidemiology of monkeypox.

00:52:51.135 --> 00:52:52.617
And when I finished, you know,

00:52:52.617 --> 00:52:53.997
in order to be able to continue this work,

00:52:53.997 --> 00:52:55.967
I was gonna need more than
half a million dollars

00:52:55.967 --> 00:52:58.435
for five years, to do this kind of work,

00:52:58.435 --> 00:53:00.267
to be able to do the epidemiology work,

00:53:00.267 --> 00:53:02.716
to be able to support the
infrastructure in DRC,

00:53:02.716 --> 00:53:04.164
to be able to do that kind of

00:53:04.164 --> 00:53:05.554
intensified disease surveillance,

00:53:05.554 --> 00:53:08.988
to be able to do the kind
of molecular epidemiology

00:53:08.988 --> 00:53:10.434
that needed to be done, to be able to have

00:53:10.434 --> 00:53:12.927
that lag capacity, and I
was like, far beyond that.

00:53:12.927 --> 00:53:16.955
And so I really wasn't gonna
be able to sustain that

00:53:16.955 --> 00:53:18.346
as a junior investigator writing

00:53:18.346 --> 00:53:21.013
my first RO1 afterwards on that.

00:53:21.865 --> 00:53:25.315
And because I didn't have
all of this infrastructure

00:53:25.315 --> 00:53:26.365
that I was plugging into,

00:53:26.365 --> 00:53:29.297
this was really infrastructure
that I developed on my own.

00:53:29.297 --> 00:53:33.514
You know, it just seemed
overwhelming and impossible to do.

00:53:33.514 --> 00:53:36.727
And so, we kind of pivoted
to other things for a while.

00:53:36.727 --> 00:53:39.148
And we'd always been working
for a low level on monkeypox,

00:53:39.148 --> 00:53:42.515
working on the data,
publishing our results,

00:53:42.515 --> 00:53:44.149
continue, you know, we had
little projects here and there

00:53:44.149 --> 00:53:45.246
to be able to continue to do

00:53:45.246 --> 00:53:48.987
active surveillance for several years.

00:53:48.987 --> 00:53:50.738
And it was only a couple
years ago that we were able

00:53:50.738 --> 00:53:54.821
to get funding to do real,
to be able to get back

00:53:58.090 --> 00:54:02.173
on the ground and really
do a more robust project

00:54:04.106 --> 00:54:08.704
on monkeypox again, just
the last couple years.

00:54:08.704 --> 00:54:09.635
And that was because there was

00:54:09.635 --> 00:54:10.824
an outbreak in Nigeria, right?

00:54:10.824 --> 00:54:13.213
So all of a sudden there
was some renewed interest

00:54:13.213 --> 00:54:17.361
that we saw this shift, this
explosion of cases there.

00:54:17.361 --> 00:54:19.514
And so all of a sudden it was
clear that this was something

00:54:19.514 --> 00:54:23.384
that needed to be paid
attention to at some level.

00:54:23.384 --> 00:54:27.031
But, you know, it's really
hard to do this kind of work

00:54:27.031 --> 00:54:30.041
in low resource settings,
and to be able to do

00:54:30.041 --> 00:54:32.043
the kind of enhanced disease surveillance

00:54:32.043 --> 00:54:33.001
that needs to happen.

00:54:33.001 --> 00:54:35.331
I mean, you know, governments
can't pay for that.

00:54:35.331 --> 00:54:38.530
The, you know, I've heard
from colleagues in Nigeria,

00:54:38.530 --> 00:54:40.553
many quotes to them
saying, you know, "listen,

00:54:40.553 --> 00:54:42.357
we knew that there were
a lot of cases out here.

00:54:42.357 --> 00:54:43.748
We couldn't do anything about it."

00:54:43.748 --> 00:54:44.895
In DRC, it's the same thing.

00:54:44.895 --> 00:54:45.896
I mean, they don't have the resources.

00:54:45.896 --> 00:54:48.227
What they're able to do is case counting,

00:54:48.227 --> 00:54:49.788
but they can't look at transmission.

00:54:49.788 --> 00:54:51.907
They can't do case investigations.

00:54:51.907 --> 00:54:54.445
It can't do all the kind of
things that need to be done.

00:54:54.445 --> 00:54:58.147
I mean, these, this is,
it's very expensive,

00:54:58.147 --> 00:55:00.118
very consuming work.

00:55:00.118 --> 00:55:03.443
And DRC has been very
consumed with a lot of other

00:55:03.443 --> 00:55:06.852
very serious outbreaks to contend
with, including, you know,

00:55:06.852 --> 00:55:09.331
back-to-back Ebola
outbreaks and other things

00:55:09.331 --> 00:55:12.070
that have really, you know, I have to,

00:55:12.070 --> 00:55:16.790
I really have to give credit
to my Congolese colleagues

00:55:16.790 --> 00:55:21.728
who are just heroes who have
worked themselves to the bone.

00:55:21.728 --> 00:55:23.950
I mean, you know, I see this burnout,

00:55:23.950 --> 00:55:26.950
and how tired people are from COVID.

00:55:27.930 --> 00:55:31.257
Well, just imagine with these
incredible people working,

00:55:31.257 --> 00:55:35.189
with many fewer doctors,
many fewer researchers,

00:55:35.189 --> 00:55:39.130
many fewer resources,
just doing incredible work

00:55:39.130 --> 00:55:41.310
on the ground with limited resources,

00:55:41.310 --> 00:55:43.188
and combating multiple outbreaks

00:55:43.188 --> 00:55:44.969
all at the same time
with very little funding

00:55:44.969 --> 00:55:46.749
and very little resource.

00:55:46.749 --> 00:55:50.491
So, I mean, I think that
this just goes back to,

00:55:50.491 --> 00:55:53.251
you pay now, or you pay later.

00:55:53.251 --> 00:55:57.106
And you gotta pick what
price you're willing to pay

00:55:57.106 --> 00:55:58.289
and when you're gonna pay it.

00:55:58.289 --> 00:55:59.367
And we've often said, "oh,

00:55:59.367 --> 00:56:00.200
well we'll pay on the back end

00:56:00.200 --> 00:56:02.717
as opposed to paying up front."

00:56:02.717 --> 00:56:07.510
And you know, that costs
us dearly in so many ways.

00:56:07.510 --> 00:56:08.843
- So one last question then to,

00:56:08.843 --> 00:56:13.641
we are sort of, before I wrap
up, and a quick question,

00:56:13.641 --> 00:56:15.220
but there's, several people are asking

00:56:15.220 --> 00:56:18.334
the same question in different ways

00:56:18.334 --> 00:56:20.952
in our question and answer box, which is,

00:56:20.952 --> 00:56:23.338
and Akiko, I'll ask you this question,

00:56:23.338 --> 00:56:26.755
should the general public get vaccinated?

00:56:27.872 --> 00:56:29.462
- Ah, well I don't...
- Against, again you know,

00:56:29.462 --> 00:56:31.323
with a smallpox vaccine, presumably,

00:56:31.323 --> 00:56:35.912
or a smallpox, an NDA vaccine
tested against monkeypox.

00:56:35.912 --> 00:56:37.133
But generally speaking,

00:56:37.133 --> 00:56:41.210
should the general public
think about getting vaccinated?

00:56:41.210 --> 00:56:43.682
- Well, at this point in the pandemic,

00:56:43.682 --> 00:56:45.630
I don't think that's quite necessary.

00:56:45.630 --> 00:56:46.953
- Don't use the P word, Akiko!

00:56:46.953 --> 00:56:48.102
I know that it was,
(Akiko laughs)

00:56:48.102 --> 00:56:49.487
this is recorded.
- Oh, I'm sorry,

00:56:49.487 --> 00:56:52.006
at this point, I'm sorry, not pandemic!

00:56:52.006 --> 00:56:55.419
At this point of the infection
we have that's going on,

00:56:55.419 --> 00:56:59.120
I don't think the general
public needs to be vaccinated.

00:56:59.120 --> 00:57:03.468
Of course, we can never
underestimate the viruses, right?

00:57:03.468 --> 00:57:06.739
There may be future variants that,

00:57:06.739 --> 00:57:08.136
even though it's unlikely,

00:57:08.136 --> 00:57:10.710
could be more transmissible or whatever.

00:57:10.710 --> 00:57:12.881
And at which point we
do need to reconsider

00:57:12.881 --> 00:57:14.721
these kinds of equations.

00:57:14.721 --> 00:57:18.930
But I think, it's not at the
point where we have to worry

00:57:18.930 --> 00:57:23.294
about the general spreading
of the virus everywhere,

00:57:23.294 --> 00:57:25.045
but we do need to really keep an eye

00:57:25.045 --> 00:57:28.664
and potentially vaccinate the people

00:57:28.664 --> 00:57:32.147
around the sort of transmission sites,

00:57:32.147 --> 00:57:34.436
and bring vaccination and other strategy

00:57:34.436 --> 00:57:37.677
that make more sense to contain the spread

00:57:37.677 --> 00:57:39.715
of the virus throughout the world.

00:57:39.715 --> 00:57:40.988
I think that that's a better approach

00:57:40.988 --> 00:57:43.571
than doing general vaccination.

00:57:44.518 --> 00:57:45.949
- So a couple of things as I wrap up.

00:57:45.949 --> 00:57:47.968
So I do believe there should be,

00:57:47.968 --> 00:57:50.539
we should build the
button, but not press it.

00:57:50.539 --> 00:57:53.719
I do believe that we need to
make investments and prepare,

00:57:53.719 --> 00:57:56.549
even with large groups,
not, you know, falling,

00:57:56.549 --> 00:57:59.882
even if you fall far short of a general,

00:58:00.717 --> 00:58:02.838
everyone getting vaccinated program.

00:58:02.838 --> 00:58:07.671
And if we have a large set of
high risk groups, et cetera,

00:58:08.696 --> 00:58:10.867
even that is a substantial investment.

00:58:10.867 --> 00:58:13.699
So we should do that absolutely now,

00:58:13.699 --> 00:58:16.808
but also we should prepare
for a few other things.

00:58:16.808 --> 00:58:19.851
First of all, because it
is the initial clusters

00:58:19.851 --> 00:58:22.924
are identified in MSM communities,

00:58:22.924 --> 00:58:26.050
we need to get ahead of
any potential stigma.

00:58:26.050 --> 00:58:30.049
We can't, you know, just
repeat disease after disease,

00:58:30.049 --> 00:58:32.896
where based on ethnicity
or sexual preference,

00:58:32.896 --> 00:58:35.209
because remember a lot of
these things are identified

00:58:35.209 --> 00:58:37.488
in certain sexual
networks, but they're not,

00:58:37.488 --> 00:58:38.321
it's not the virus.

00:58:38.321 --> 00:58:42.017
It's the initial identification
that is happening.

00:58:42.017 --> 00:58:42.850
So we don't know, so I mean,

00:58:42.850 --> 00:58:45.229
there may be certain
risk factors associated

00:58:45.229 --> 00:58:48.456
with different kind of
contact, but we need

00:58:48.456 --> 00:58:51.298
to address stigma, because
it's in all our interest

00:58:51.298 --> 00:58:53.817
that we get ahead of this virus by,

00:58:53.817 --> 00:58:56.038
through collaboration of, in cooperation

00:58:56.038 --> 00:58:57.747
of various communities.

00:58:57.747 --> 00:58:59.698
And it is a moral
responsibility to address it

00:58:59.698 --> 00:59:02.879
where, you know, where it is impacting.

00:59:02.879 --> 00:59:05.501
The other thing is we need to
get ahead of misinformation.

00:59:05.501 --> 00:59:08.586
And we need to do that, and we need

00:59:08.586 --> 00:59:11.107
to have a robust research agenda.

00:59:11.107 --> 00:59:13.328
And I, we received our group received

00:59:13.328 --> 00:59:16.358
the first IRB approval of our first study

00:59:16.358 --> 00:59:18.370
on this series of outbreaks two days ago.

00:59:18.370 --> 00:59:21.139
And so we're already in
the field, et cetera.

00:59:21.139 --> 00:59:23.147
Not in the field in DRC, et cetera,

00:59:23.147 --> 00:59:24.893
but it's a different kind of study.

00:59:24.893 --> 00:59:27.602
But, so, we'll have to
have this robust agenda.

00:59:27.602 --> 00:59:30.900
With that, I'll wrap
up, with only 50 minute,

00:59:30.900 --> 00:59:33.973
50 seconds over our time, I'll wrap up.

00:59:33.973 --> 00:59:38.162
Stay tuned for our fall season
of the conversation series

00:59:38.162 --> 00:59:41.162
and likely to have, and we know that

00:59:42.811 --> 00:59:45.239
with the speakers we are lining up

00:59:45.239 --> 00:59:47.699
we'll attract a lot of interest.

00:59:47.699 --> 00:59:50.512
So look forward to that, and just repeat,

00:59:50.512 --> 00:59:53.947
Akiko did not mean to say "pandemic"

00:59:53.947 --> 00:59:55.454
at this point.
- [Akiko] Sorry.

00:59:55.454 --> 00:59:57.569
- And, but things can be highly disruptive

00:59:57.569 --> 01:00:01.191
even before, without
being a full-on pandemic.

01:00:01.191 --> 01:00:04.868
With that, thank you, and
thanks for joining us.

01:00:04.868 --> 01:00:07.899
And obviously I couldn't
get to all the questions,

01:00:07.899 --> 01:00:09.269
and they were really nice questions.

01:00:09.269 --> 01:00:11.794
I wish I had the time.

01:00:11.794 --> 01:00:12.644
- [Nate] Thank you.
- [Saad] My pleasure.

01:00:12.644 --> 01:00:13.477
- [Akiko] Thank you.

01:00:13.477 --> 01:00:15.060
- [Anne] Thank you!

